/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-1","number":1,"title":"Sets Cloud","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"1.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-1-1-101","code":"1.1","title":"1.1","blurb":"1.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-1-1","title":"1.1.1 — + New button","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.1 — + New button","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create a blank Set from the Sets Library and land in Session View so you can start a sketch immediately."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every time you open Note you land on the Sets Library. At the top of that screen is <strong>+ New</strong>. Tap it and Note creates a Set, opens it, and shows Session View: tracks as vertical columns, empty clip slots under the track headers, and the transport at the bottom."},{"type":"prose","html":"A new Set is not an empty rack. You get three default tracks so you can play drums, a bass sound, and a melodic sound without browsing first. Later lessons cover what those tracks actually load. The important mechanic here is the one tap: <strong>+ New</strong> both creates the project and opens it. You do not pick a template dialog or a file name first."},{"type":"prose","html":"That speed is the musical reason this control exists. Note is built for sketching ideas anywhere. If a rhythm or bass line appears while you are away from Live, you open the app, tap <strong>+ New</strong>, and you are on pads in a few seconds. Name the Set later from Set Options. Auto-save will keep the file on the device; you do not need a Save command to create it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note and stay on the Sets Library. Do not tap an existing Set.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","Confirm Session View opened: three track headers across the top and a grid of empty clip slots beneath them.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name and confirm the new Set now appears in the library list.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong> again from memory, then return to the library and count how many new Sets you just made."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two things does <strong>+ New</strong> do in one tap? <strong>Answer:</strong> It creates a new Set and opens that Set in Session View.","How many tracks does a newly created Set show? <strong>Answer:</strong> Three default tracks.","Do you have to name the Set before it exists? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The Set is created immediately. You rename it later from Set Options."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.1 Creating New Sets","Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View; §4.1 Tracks"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create one Set with <strong>+ New</strong>. Leave it open long enough to see the three tracks, then return to the library. Rename can wait until Module 1.3. For the course, treat this file as the start of <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-2","title":"1.1.2 — Default tracks preloaded with drum/bass/melodic/sampler presets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.2 — Default tracks preloaded with drum/bass/melodic/sampler presets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize the three tracks that appear in a new Set and name the preset families Note can preload on them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap <strong>+ New</strong> and you see three tracks. The official manual states each of those tracks is preloaded with a drum, bass, melodic instrument, or Sampler preset. You are not looking at empty instrument slots. You are looking at playable Track Presets so a sketch can start on the pads without a browser trip."},{"type":"prose","html":"Those four families are the useful map. A drum preload gives you a Drum Rack style kit. A bass or other melodic preload gives you a pitched instrument. A Sampler preload puts a sample-based instrument on the track. You confirm which family you got by tapping a track header, which opens Instrument View and shows the loaded Track Preset name at the top."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not treat the three tracks as a locked song template. You can swap any preset from the browser once you are in Instrument View. The default load exists so the first idea is musical, not administrative. If you always want a different starting kit, change the preset after the Set opens rather than hunting for a hidden new-Set template. Note does not document a user-defined new-Set template."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From the Sets Library, tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","In Session View, count the track headers. Confirm there are three.","Tap the first track header. Read the Track Preset name at the top of Instrument View and decide whether it is a drum, bass, other melodic, or Sampler sound.","Tap the back arrow to return to Session View. Repeat for the second and third tracks.","Play a few pads on each track with the transport stopped, then say the three families you actually received without looking at the names."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many tracks does a new Set contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> Three.","What can those tracks be preloaded with? <strong>Answer:</strong> A drum, bass, melodic instrument, or Sampler preset.","Are those preloads permanent for the life of the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You can replace any Track Preset from the browser after the Set opens."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.1 Creating New Sets","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks","Ableton Note Manual §6 Instrument View","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong>, write a one-line label for each of the three default tracks (for example: drums / bass / keys). Play one short phrase on each track. Do not add a fourth track yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-3","title":"1.1.3 — Random Track Preset on new tracks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.3 — Random Track Preset on new tracks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Explain why a new track does not always load the same sound, and replace that random Track Preset when you need a specific role."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.1 is explicit: each track in a Set comes loaded with a random Track Preset. A Track Preset is not just an instrument. It is an instrument plus two audio effects, with the instrument type shown by the icon on the first tab: <strong>Drift</strong>, <strong>Drum Rack</strong>, <strong>Melodic Sampler</strong>, or <strong>Wavetable</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That random load applies when Note creates tracks for you. <strong>+ New</strong> therefore hands you three playable tracks whose exact presets change from Set to Set, even though the families stay in the drum / bass / melodic / Sampler range described in §3.1. If you later swipe left and tap <strong>Add Track</strong> (maximum eight tracks), the new column also arrives with a Track Preset already on it."},{"type":"prose","html":"As of Note 2.0.1 the <strong>Add Track</strong> control offers <strong>Beat</strong>, <strong>Synth</strong>, and <strong>Audio</strong> buttons. That is a version-gated chooser for the <em>kind</em> of track. It does not cancel the Track Preset idea: a Beat or Synth track still opens as a preset you can swap. Do not import 2.x audio-track rules into a pre-2.0 install."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musically this is a feature, not a bug. Random starting sounds stop you from rebuilding the same kit every session. When the random pick is wrong for the idea, tap the preset name and load a known preset from the Core Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong> or tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","Write down the three Track Preset names you see.","Swipe left and tap <strong>Add Track</strong>. On Note 2.0.1+, choose <strong>Beat</strong> or <strong>Synth</strong> if those buttons appear.","Open the new track and read its preset name. Confirm it is already loaded, not empty.","Tap the preset name, preview a different preset in the same family, tap <strong>Done</strong>, then play both the random original (undo if needed) and the one you chose."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a Track Preset contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> One instrument plus two audio effects.","Does every new track start empty? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Each track comes loaded with a random Track Preset.","Which four instrument types can sit inside a Track Preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.1 Creating New Sets","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Beat / Synth / Audio add-track buttons)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add one extra track to LAB-01. If the random preset is not a drum, bass, or lead you can use, replace it from the browser. Stay at or under eight tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-4","title":"1.1.4 — The back arrow returns to the Sets Library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.4 — The back arrow returns to the Sets Library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave an open Set and return to the Sets Library without losing the Set, using the back arrow at the left of the Set name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Session View puts the Set name in the top bar. To the left of that name is a back arrow. Tap it and Note closes Session View and returns you to the Sets Library. The Set stays on the device. You are not deleting or exporting anything."},{"type":"prose","html":"That same arrow is context-sensitive. From Instrument View — the pad and device page you reach by tapping a track header, double-tapping a clip, or tapping <strong>Add Clip</strong> — the back arrow returns you to Session View, not all the way to the library. One more tap on the arrow in Session View takes you home to the list of Sets."},{"type":"prose","html":"Learn the two-step path now, before Cloud and Share To… add more exits. The library is where you preview, rename, duplicate, upload, and share. Session View is where you play. If you stay inside a Set looking for those management commands, you are in the wrong place. Go back to the library, then use the three-dots Set Options menu on that Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Because Note auto-saves in the background, leaving a Set with the back arrow does not require a Save tap. Treat the arrow as the normal way out."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong> so Session View is visible.","Tap a track header to enter Instrument View. Confirm pads (or Keys) are on screen.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name. Confirm you are in Session View, not the library.","Tap the back arrow again. Confirm the Sets Library is showing and LAB-01 is still in the list.","Re-open the Set from the library without looking at this page, then walk Instrument View → Session View → library once more."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the control that leaves a Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> The back arrow to the left of the Set name in Session View.","What does that arrow do from Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> It returns you to Session View. A second tap from Session View returns you to the Sets Library.","Does tapping the back arrow delete the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The Set remains in the library (Local, or Cloud if you have uploaded it)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View (Back Arrow to Sets Library)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (Back Arrow to Session View)","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note (auto-save)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From LAB-01, enter Instrument View, return to Session View, then return to the library. Time yourself. Repeat until the two-tap path is automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-5","title":"1.1.5 — Demo Sets for listening study","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.5 — Demo Sets for listening study","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the included Demo Sets as listening references without treating them as your working project or as Cloud uploads."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Sets Library is not only your own work. When you open Note you also see Demo Sets that ship with the app. The manual describes them as part of the library alongside <strong>+ New</strong> and the Sets you have created. They exist so you can hear how a finished Note sketch is built: tracks, scenes, and sounds already in place."},{"type":"prose","html":"As of Note 2.0.1 the Core Library adds Demo Sets by Alice Ivy, Jose Castillo, and Heavy Mellow. Those particular Demos showcase audio tracks, which are a 2.0 feature. On a pre-2.0 install you still get Demo Sets; the named 2.0.1 trio and their audio-track content are version-gated."},{"type":"prose","html":"Listen first. Preview from the library play button, then open a Demo and launch scenes. Notice how many tracks are used, how scenes change the arrangement, and which instrument families carry drums, bass, and harmony. Then go back to your own Set and steal the <em>structure</em>, not the file."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not use a Demo as LAB-01. Work in a Set you created. Release notes 1.0.12 fixed a bug that occurred when a user tried to upload a Demo Set to Cloud, which is a hint that Demos are not your Cloud transfer objects. Keep Cloud slots for Sets you own."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the Sets Library, locate a Demo Set. Do not tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","Tap the play button next to the Demo name and listen without opening the Set.","Stop the preview, then tap the Demo itself to open Session View.","Launch two or three scenes and count the tracks in use. Write one sentence about how the Demo is arranged.","Tap the back arrow to the library. Confirm your own LAB-01 is still a separate row from the Demo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do Demo Sets appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Sets Library, together with <strong>+ New</strong> and the Sets you have created.","What did Note 2.0.1 add to the Demo list? <strong>Answer:</strong> Demo Sets by Alice Ivy, Jose Castillo, and Heavy Mellow that showcase audio tracks.","Should you upload a Demo to occupy a Cloud slot for this course? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Use a Set you created. Cloud is for transferring your work."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Core Library Updates (named Demo Sets)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (Demo Set Cloud upload bug fix)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview one Demo, then open it and launch every scene once. Write three observations (track count, scene count, one sound you want to recreate). Recreate none of it in the Demo. Recreate one idea later in LAB-01."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-6","title":"1.1.6 — Note 2.x: last key/tempo remembered on new Sets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.6 — Note 2.x: last key/tempo remembered on new Sets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1 or later, predict the key and tempo of a newly created Set from the last Set you used."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1 changed how new Sets start. The last used key and tempo are remembered when you create a new Set. If you finish a sketch in A minor at 94 BPM, the next <strong>+ New</strong> Set comes back in that key and tempo instead of forcing you to re-enter both from Set Settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is version-gated. The local Note manual is the pre-2.0 surface. It does not describe memory of the last key and tempo. On a 1.x install, set tempo and key per Set from the Set Settings icon at the top right of Session View. Do not expect 2.x memory behavior there."},{"type":"prose","html":"Check your installed version before you rely on this: App Settings (three dots at the bottom-left of the Sets Library) → <strong>Help</strong>. If you are on 2.0.1 or later, treat last-key / last-tempo as a workflow feature. It keeps a body of sketches in the same pocket so you can jump from Set to Set without resetting the room. It also means a leftover 140 BPM session will follow you into the next sketch unless you change it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Key and tempo still live in Set Settings after the Set exists. Memory only seeds the new file. You can always override both."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open App Settings → <strong>Help</strong> and write down the Note version.","If you are on 2.0.1 or later, open LAB-01, tap the Set Settings icon, set a distinctive tempo (for example 94) and a distinctive key, then return to the library.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong>. Open Set Settings on that new Set and confirm the tempo and key match what you just used.","Change the new Set to a different tempo, return to the library, tap <strong>+ New</strong> again, and confirm the newest Set followed the second tempo.","If you are on a pre-2.0 version, set tempo and key manually on two Sets and write <code>{type:'gap'}</code>: last key/tempo memory is a 2.0.1 feature and is not in your build."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Starting in which Note version are last used key and tempo remembered on a new Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1.","Where do you still change key and tempo after the Set exists? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings, via the icon to the right of the Set name.","Does this behavior appear in the pre-2.0 Note manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The manual is the pre-2.0 surface. Use the 2.0.1 release notes for this fact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Improvements (last used key and tempo remembered)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo; §5.3 Key &amp; Scale (where you still set them)","Note FAQ, The Note App (Settings → Help for version)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you are on 2.0.1+, set LAB-01 to a tempo you actually want for the course and a key you want to stay in. Create one extra Set and confirm it inherited both. Delete that extra Set only after you have a backup path from later lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-1-7","title":"1.1.7 — Note 2.x: C Major default key; piano layout default melodic","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.7 — Note 2.x: C Major default key; piano layout default melodic","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.1 — Creating Sets</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State the Note 2.0.1 defaults for key and melodic layout, and contrast them with the pre-2.0 manual defaults."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Two defaults changed in Note 2.0.1: C Major is the default key for new Sets, and piano layout is the default for melodic tracks. Combined with the previous lesson, a fresh 2.x Set starts in C Major at the last used tempo unless you already changed the last key, and new melodic tracks present a piano-style layout instead of the older pad grid default."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pre-2.0 manual says something different and you must not blend the two. In that surface, the <strong>In Key</strong> switch is off by default, so pads play a Western chromatic scale until you enable <strong>In Key</strong> and pick a root and scale. Melodic pads default to a 5×5 grid; <strong>Keys</strong> is a separate two-octave keyboard. When <strong>In Key</strong> is on, Keys become equal rectangles; when it is off, Keys stay a chromatic piano."},{"type":"prose","html":"So the honest rule is dated. On 2.0.1+: expect C Major and a piano layout on new melodic tracks. On the manual / 1.x surface: expect chromatic (<strong>In Key</strong> off) and the 5×5 pad default. Always read the version on <strong>Help</strong>. Last used key still overrides the C Major default on 2.x once you have changed key in a previous Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical reason to care is pad geography. A piano layout rewards people who already think in keyboard shapes. A 5×5 In-Key grid rewards people who want only scale tones. Know which one you were handed before you decide a melody “won’t sit.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm your Note version under App Settings → <strong>Help</strong>.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong> (or open a Set you have not customized).","Open Set Settings → <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>. Write down the current key and whether <strong>In Key</strong> is on.","Open a melodic track and switch between the pad layout and <strong>Keys</strong>. Note whether you see a piano keyboard or equal rectangles.","From memory, say the 2.0.1 defaults and the pre-2.0 manual defaults as two separate sentences."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default key for new Sets as of Note 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> C Major.","What is the default layout for new melodic tracks as of Note 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Piano layout.","What is the pre-2.0 Key &amp; Scale default in the manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>In Key</strong> is off, so the pads are chromatic until you enable the switch and choose a root and scale."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Improvements (C Major default; piano layout default)","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 Key &amp; Scale","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 Pad Performance Options (melodic pads / Keys)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a new Set, open <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> and play the preview pads in that menu. Then open a melodic track in <strong>Keys</strong> and play the same tonic. Write one line: which layout you will use for LAB-01 and why."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-1-2-102","code":"1.2","title":"1.2","blurb":"1.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-2-1","title":"1.2.1 — Library sections: Local vs Cloud vs Demo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.1 — Library sections: Local vs Cloud vs Demo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Point to the three kinds of Sets in the library and say what each location actually means for storage."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Sets Library is the home screen. It holds <strong>+ New</strong>, the Sets you have already made, and Demo Sets. The manual splits <em>your</em> Sets by storage: Sets uploaded to Ableton Cloud appear in the Ableton Cloud section; Sets that live only on this device appear in the <strong>Local</strong> section. Demos are included with the app and sit in the same library as listening references."},{"type":"prose","html":"Those three labels are not three copies of the same file type in three folders you manage by hand. <strong>Local</strong> means the Set has not been uploaded. <strong>Cloud</strong> means you sent that Set to Ableton Cloud, so it can appear in Live’s browser and on other Note installs signed into the same ableton.com account. The local project still remains on the device under Files; Cloud is a transfer lane, not a second original. Demos are factory content. Do not treat a Demo as a Cloud slot or as LAB-01."},{"type":"prose","html":"If Cloud is not enabled, you will not see the Cloud area as an active destination for your own Sets. Enable Cloud later in Module 1.6. For this lesson, learn to read the library: which rows are yours and local, which rows are yours and uploaded, and which rows are Demos."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note to the Sets Library.","Find <strong>+ New</strong>, then scan the list and mark three kinds of rows on paper: Local Sets you created, Cloud Sets if any exist, and Demo Sets.","Tap play on one Demo, then stop it. Do not upload anything.","Confirm LAB-01 is in <strong>Local</strong> unless you already uploaded it.","From memory, define Local, Cloud, and Demo in one sentence each."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do Sets that exist only on this iPhone or iPad appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the <strong>Local</strong> section of the Sets Library.","Where do uploaded Sets appear in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Ableton Cloud section of the Sets Library.","What are Demo Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Factory Sets included with the app for reference and listening, listed in the same library."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud with Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview a Demo without opening it for edit. Then point at LAB-01 and say whether it is Local or Cloud. Leave it Local until you intentionally upload a test Set in Module 1.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-2-2","title":"1.2.2 — Play button previews a Set","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.2 — Play button previews a Set","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview a Set from the library play button so you can identify it by ear without opening Session View."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Each Set row in the library has a play button next to the name. Tap that button and Note starts playback of the Set. You stay on the Sets Library. You do not enter Session View, you do not land on pads, and you do not start editing clips."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the whole control. Use it to tell two similarly named sketches apart, to audition a Demo, or to check that yesterday’s file is the one you think it is before you open it. Early release notes fixed erratic play-button behavior in the library, which is further evidence that preview is a first-class library action, not an accident."},{"type":"prose","html":"Preview is not a substitute for opening the Set. You cannot capture, rename from inside the Set, or change devices from the play button. You also cannot treat preview as an export. When you need to work, open the Set. When you need to listen and stay in the list, tap play."},{"type":"prose","html":"If several Sets look the same because they still have default artwork, preview plus a clear name is how you find the right row as the library grows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Stay on the Sets Library.","Tap the play button next to a Demo Set. Confirm you are still on the library screen while audio plays.","Stop the preview.","Tap play on LAB-01 (or another Set you created). Listen for a few seconds, then stop.","Repeat the Demo preview from memory. Do not open either Set in this lesson."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the play button next to a Set name do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It starts playback of that Set from the library.","Does tapping play open Session View? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Playback happens from the Sets Library.","When do you use play instead of opening the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> When you only need to hear which Set it is, or to study a Demo without editing it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (library play-button fix, official RN)","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (preview without opening)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview one Demo and LAB-01 back to back without opening either. Write which file you would open if you had only ten minutes to continue a sketch, and why the preview was enough to decide."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-2-3","title":"1.2.3 — Open Set vs preview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.3 — Open Set vs preview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose preview when you only need to hear a Set, and open the Set when you need Session View."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The library gives you two different entries into a Set. The play button starts playback and leaves you on the list. Opening the Set takes you into Session View, where tracks, clips, scenes, the mixer, and Set Settings live."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual describes the play button as the way to start playback of a Set from the library. It describes Session View as what you see <em>when you open a Set</em>. Those are separate actions. Preview answers “which file is this?” Open answers “I need to play pads, capture, or change the Set.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not name the exact hit-target that opens a Set (the name, the artwork, or the whole row minus the play button). On your device, tap the Set itself rather than the play button and confirm Session View appears. Use that gesture as Open."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not edit from a preview. If you tap play and then look for <strong>Add Clip</strong> or the back arrow, you are still in the library. Stop the preview, open the Set, do the work, then use the back arrow to return. Mixing up the two gestures is how people think a Set “won’t open” when they have only been previewing it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the Sets Library, tap play on LAB-01. Confirm Session View did <strong>not</strong> open.","Stop the preview.","Tap the Set (not the play button) and confirm Session View opens.","Tap the back arrow to return to the library.","Do the pair again from memory: preview, stop, open, back."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the difference between play and open? <strong>Answer:</strong> Play previews the Set from the library. Open enters Session View so you can work.","Where do you capture clips and change devices? <strong>Answer:</strong> Inside an opened Set (Session View / Instrument View), not from the library play button.","How do you leave an opened Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (preview without opening)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview a Demo, stop it, then open LAB-01 and add nothing. The drill is the gesture pair, not new music. Repeat until you can do it without thinking about which control is which."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-2-4","title":"1.2.4 — Cloud area after activation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.4 — Cloud area after activation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize the Ableton Cloud area of the Sets Library after Cloud is enabled, and know what can appear there before you upload anything of your own."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton Cloud is off until you enable it. The toggle lives in App Settings, in an <strong>Enable Ableton Cloud</strong> area of the Set Library, and on <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> inside Set Options. Turning the toggle on opens ableton.com in a browser so you can connect the service to your Ableton user account. An account is required for Cloud; it is not required to use Note itself."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once Cloud is active, the Sets Library gains (or fills) an Ableton Cloud section. Two kinds of Sets show up there. First, any Set you upload from this device moves from <strong>Local</strong> into that Cloud section. Second, Sets already stored in Cloud from other Ableton products — the manual’s example is Move — appear in the same area. You do not re-download them by hand."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you see Cloud Sets you did not create on this iPhone or iPad, that is expected on a shared account. They belong to the same eight-Set Cloud capacity as everything else. Do not delete someone else’s in-progress transfer unless you intend to free a slot. If Cloud is not enabled, you will only see Local and Demo rows, which is also expected."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is identification, not the full upload workflow. Module 1.6 covers login, the eight-Set cap, and Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open App Settings from the three dots at the bottom-left of the Sets Library.","Find the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle. Note whether it is on or off. Do not change it unless you are ready to sign in.","Return to the library and look for an <strong>Enable Ableton Cloud</strong> banner or an Ableton Cloud section.","If Cloud is already on, list every Set in the Cloud area and mark which ones you created on this device.","From memory, name the three places the Cloud toggle can be reached."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What appears in the Cloud area after activation besides Sets you upload from this device? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sets already stored in Cloud, for example from Move, signed in with the same account.","Do you need an Ableton account to use Note at all? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You need an account to use Ableton Cloud.","After you upload a Set, where does it appear in Note’s library? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Ableton Cloud section, not in Local."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud with Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Without uploading, write whether Cloud is on for your install and how many Sets already sit in the Cloud area. If the area is empty or missing, write “Cloud off or unused” and continue. You upload a test Set in 1.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-2-5","title":"1.2.5 — Sorting/finding Sets as library grows","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.5 — Sorting/finding Sets as library grows","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find a Set in a growing library using the controls the sources actually document: sections, names, artwork, and preview."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official extracts do not document a sort menu, a filter, or a search field on the Sets Library list. Do not invent A–Z, date, or “recents” controls. Find Sets with what is specified."},{"type":"prose","html":"The library is already grouped. <strong>Local</strong> holds Sets that live only on this device. <strong>Cloud</strong> holds uploaded Sets. Demo Sets are factory content. Start by looking in the right section. A Set you never uploaded will not be in Cloud. A Demo will never be LAB-01."},{"type":"prose","html":"Inside a section, the documented identifiers are the Set name, the artwork, and the play button. Rename from Set Options so the row is readable (Module 1.3). Choose artwork so two sketches with similar names do not look identical. Tap play to confirm by ear before you open the wrong file."},{"type":"prose","html":"A course prefix is a naming habit, not an official sort key. Calling the working file <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong> and a safety duplicate <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong> is how you stay oriented when the list gets long. Note will not file those prefixes for you. You read the list."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you still cannot find a Set, it may live in Files under <strong>On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → Projects</strong> (Module 1.4), or it may have been deleted from the device. Cloud will not magically archive every old Local Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Sets Library and count your Local Sets, Cloud Sets, and Demos.","Preview two Sets with similar names or default artwork. Stop each preview before opening.","Open Set Options on LAB-01 and confirm the current name is distinctive. Rename it in the next module if it is not.","Scan Cloud and Local separately for the same title so you do not open the wrong section’s row.","From memory, list the four documented ways to identify a Set: section, name, artwork, play."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is there an official library sort or search control in the extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That UI is not documented. Finding Sets uses sections, names, artwork, and preview.","Where do you look first for a Set you have never uploaded? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>Local</strong> section.","What is the fastest sourced way to tell two similar rows apart without opening them? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the play button and listen."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library Overview","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options (rename, artwork)","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview a Demo without opening it for edit. Then find LAB-01 by section and name only, and preview it once to confirm it is the working file."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-2-6","title":"1.2.6 — Widgets / home-screen artwork behavior","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.6 — Widgets / home-screen artwork behavior","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.2 — Sets Library map</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State what official release notes actually say about Note widgets and artwork, and avoid undocumented widget workflows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note can appear as an iOS home-screen widget. The local release-notes extract in this course folder does not describe how to add that widget or what every size does. Official Note Release Notes still mention widgets in bug fixes, which is enough to treat them as a real surface:"},{"type":"list","items":["Note 1.0.15: changing artwork did not update widgets (fixed). After that version, a new Set image should show on the widget.","Note 1.3: the widget did not display correctly with tinted home-screen colors (fixed)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The assigned extract folder does not document widget sizes, the iOS Add Widget steps, lock-screen placement, or a “create new Set from the widget” control. An Ableton blog post outside this folder describes adding widgets to keep Sets within reach and creating new Sets from the home or lock screen. Treat those extra steps as unofficial for this course until they appear in the extract set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Widgets do not replace the Sets Library, Cloud, or Files. They do not back up a Set. They do not change clip data. Artwork is cosmetic identity: it helps you recognize a Set on the library row and, after the 1.0.15 fix, on the widget. Change artwork from Set Options. Confirm the widget only after you have set artwork on a Set you own."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Check App Settings → <strong>Help</strong> for your Note version.","If you already have a Note widget on the Home Screen, open LAB-01’s Set Options, note the current artwork, and leave it unchanged until Lesson 1.3.4.","If you have no widget, do not invent a layout. Write <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for add-widget steps and continue using the Sets Library.","After you set artwork in Module 1.3, return here and see whether an existing widget updated. On 1.0.15+ it should.","Say out loud: a widget is a launcher and a picture, not a backup."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 1.0.15 fix about widgets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Changing a Set’s artwork now updates the widget.","Do widgets store a second copy of the Set in iCloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No sourced claim says that. Widgets are a home-screen surface, not a backup system.","Where do you change the picture a widget should reflect? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Options → choose artwork from the Photo Library or take a photo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15 Fixes (Widgets: changing artwork did not update widgets)","Note Release Notes 1.3 Fixes (widget / tinted home-screen colors)","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options (artwork)","{type:'gap'} Widget add/create workflow not in the assigned extracts"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Look at your Home Screen. If a Note widget is there, write whether it shows LAB-01’s artwork. If none is there, skip adding one and preview a Demo from the Sets Library instead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-1-3-103","code":"1.3","title":"1.3","blurb":"1.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-3-1","title":"1.3.1 — Three-dots Set Options menu","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.1 — Three-dots Set Options menu","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Set Options from a library row and name every documented command in that menu."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Management does not live inside Session View. On the Sets Library, each Set has a play button and, next to it, three dots. Tap those dots to open <strong>Set Options</strong>. That menu is where you duplicate, rename, attach artwork, upload to Cloud, and share."},{"type":"prose","html":"The official manual lists these actions: duplicate; rename; choose artwork from your Photo Library or take a photo; <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>; and <strong>Share To…</strong>. <strong>Share To…</strong> is the path to AirDrop or other apps, copying the Set, or saving it to the Files app. It creates an <code>.ablbundle</code>. A secondary extract also lists <strong>Delete</strong>, which permanently removes the Set from the device."},{"type":"prose","html":"Learn the menu as a map before you use any single command in anger. <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> is a transfer into an eight-Set Cloud, not a backup. <strong>Share To…</strong> is the backup and collaborator path. <strong>Duplicate</strong> is the safe way to branch a sketch. <strong>Delete</strong> is last, and only after a copy exists."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse this menu with App Settings. App Settings is the three dots at the <em>bottom-left</em> of the Sets Library (Cloud toggle, Help, Preferences). Set Options is the three dots <em>on a Set row</em>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the Sets Library, find LAB-01. Do not tap play.","Tap the three dots next to that Set’s play button.","Read every row in the menu. Write the list on paper.","Close the menu without changing anything.","Open it again from memory and point to <strong>Duplicate</strong>, <strong>Rename</strong>, artwork, <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>, <strong>Share To…</strong>, and <strong>Delete</strong> if shown."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you open Set Options? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the three dots next to the play button on a Set in the Sets Library.","Name five documented Set Options actions. <strong>Answer:</strong> Duplicate, Rename, choose artwork / take a photo, Upload to Ableton Cloud, Share To…. Delete is also listed in the secondary extract.","How is this different from the three dots at the bottom-left of the library? <strong>Answer:</strong> Those open App Settings, not Set Options."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note Manual §2 App Settings (library bottom-left three dots)","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Delete listed on Set Options)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Set Options on LAB-01 and on one Demo. Close both without deleting. The skill is hitting the correct three-dots control on the first try."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-2","title":"1.3.2 — Duplicate","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.2 — Duplicate","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make an exact copy of a Set from Set Options so you can branch a sketch or keep a safety copy on the device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Duplicate</strong> sits in Set Options, the three dots next to a Set’s play button. It creates an exact copy of the selected Set. The new row appears in the library as its own Set. You can rename it, change its artwork, and keep working without touching the original clips."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Duplicate in two musical situations. First, before a risky edit: you like yesterday’s drums and you are about to rewrite the bass. Duplicate, rename the copy, experiment there. Second, as a starting point. Note has no documented user template for <strong>+ New</strong>. If you build a track layout you like, duplicate that Set the next time you start, then replace the clips. That habit is a workflow, not an official template feature."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate is local. It does not upload the copy to Cloud and it does not create an <code>.ablbundle</code>. If the original is already in Cloud, do not assume the duplicate occupies a second Cloud slot until you upload it. Treat the copy as another Local Set until you decide otherwise."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate is not <strong>Share To…</strong> and it is not the <strong>Copy</strong> option inside <strong>Share To…</strong>. Those are later lessons. For this lesson, one tap: <strong>Duplicate</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong>.","Tap <strong>Duplicate</strong>.","Confirm a second Set appears in the library.","Open Set Options on the copy and rename it <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong> (Lesson 1.3.3 covers illegal characters if the rename fails).","Preview the original and the copy. Confirm they sound the same, then leave the original closed while you work on later lessons."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Duplicate</strong> create? <strong>Answer:</strong> An exact copy of the selected Set, as its own library row.","Does Duplicate upload the copy to Ableton Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Upload is a separate Set Options command.","Why duplicate before a rewrite? <strong>Answer:</strong> So you can change the copy and still open the original sketch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Duplicate as a copy / informal template habit)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate LAB-01 and keep the copy as <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong>. Do not delete either file. Set artwork on the working Set in the next lessons so you can tell them apart at a glance."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-3","title":"1.3.3 — Rename (illegal characters / Cloud rules)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.3 — Rename (illegal characters / Cloud rules)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Rename a Set from Set Options and keep the name legal for Ableton Cloud, including the documented forbidden colon."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Rename</strong> is in Set Options. Use it as soon as a sketch is worth finding again. A library full of default names is how you open the wrong Set and overwrite the right idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud adds a naming rule. Note 1.0.12 added <code>:</code> (colon) to the forbidden character list for Ableton Cloud. Do not put a colon in a Set name you might upload. The same release notes mention an “illegal character” message in other languages, which confirms that a forbidden-character list exists beyond that one fix."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not publish the complete list of illegal characters. Do not invent a full allowed alphabet. If Note rejects a name, remove punctuation and try again. Prefer letters, numbers, spaces, and hyphens because those have not been called out as forbidden. Avoid colon for certain."},{"type":"prose","html":"Renaming the Set in Note is not the same as renaming an <code>.ablbundle</code> on macOS after <strong>Share To…</strong>. The troubleshooting article covers broken Finder names when you share the same file into one folder repeatedly. Fix those on the computer. In Note, rename from Set Options before you upload."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on your working Set.","Tap <strong>Rename</strong> and set the name to <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong> if it is not already.","Open Set Options on the duplicate and name it <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong>.","Do <strong>not</strong> put a colon in either name. If you want to see the guard, try a throwaway Set named <code>TEST:BAD</code>, then rename it without the colon.","Preview both renamed Sets and confirm the library rows match the names you typed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which character did Note 1.0.12 add to the Cloud forbidden list? <strong>Answer:</strong> The colon (<code>:</code>).","Where do you rename a Set inside Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Options → <strong>Rename</strong>.","Is the full illegal-character list published in the course extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Only the colon addition is specified. If a name is rejected, simplify it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 Fixes (Ableton Cloud: added “:” to forbidden character list)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (illegal-character message)","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (macOS Share To… name damage — different from in-app Rename)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Rename the working Set and its duplicate as <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong> and <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong>. No colons. Then set artwork so the two rows also look different."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-4","title":"1.3.4 — Artwork from Photo Library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.4 — Artwork from Photo Library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Assign a still image from the device Photo Library as a Set’s artwork so the library row is recognizable."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Options lets you choose artwork for a Set from your Photo Library. The picture becomes the Set’s thumbnail in the Sets Library. It does not change clips, samples, tempo, or Cloud status. It is a visual label."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it when two Sets have similar names or when you want the working file to stand out from the backup duplicate. A photo of a notebook page, a color field, or a location from the session is enough. The sources do not specify required pixel size, file type, or a crop tool. {type:'gap'} Exact image-format limits and editing controls for Set artwork are not documented in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"After Note 1.0.15, changing artwork should also update a Home Screen widget if you use one. That is a display fix, not a reason to pick artwork. Pick artwork so <em>you</em> can find the Set in the library."},{"type":"prose","html":"iOS may ask for Photos access the first time you choose artwork. Grant it, or the picker cannot open. That permission is separate from microphone access, which Note requests for sampling."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong>.","Choose the command that picks artwork from the Photo Library.","Select an existing image. Confirm the library row now shows that picture.","Leave the BACKUP Set on default artwork, or pick a clearly different image, so the two rows do not match.","Return to the library and find LAB-01 by artwork alone, then preview it to confirm you picked the working file."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you assign Photo Library artwork? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Options on the Sets Library row.","Does artwork change the music in the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a thumbnail for identification.","What did Note 1.0.15 fix about artwork and widgets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Changing artwork now updates widgets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (widgets update when artwork changes)","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Choose Artwork)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put a distinctive Photo Library image on LAB-01. Keep LAB-01-BACKUP visually different. Find the working Set twice: once by name, once by artwork."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-5","title":"1.3.5 — Artwork from camera","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.5 — Artwork from camera","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a new photo with the device camera and use it as Set artwork when you do not want to pick from the Photo Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Options also lets you take a photo to use as artwork. The camera path is for the moment you are actually making the sketch: shoot the desk, the venue, the lyric scrap, or a color that matches the idea, and the library thumbnail becomes a memory cue."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is still cosmetic. The photo does not become a sample. Sampling uses the microphone and Drum Rack or Sampler. Artwork uses the camera for a still image on the Set row. Keep those two captures separate in your head."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15 fixed a blank camera screen when camera rights had not been granted beforehand. If the viewfinder is empty, open the iOS Settings app → Privacy &amp; Security → Camera and allow Note. That is the same class of system permission as microphone access, pointed at a different sensor."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not say whether the camera shot is saved only on the Set or also added to the Photo Library, and they do not describe flash, front-camera, or crop controls. Use whatever the system camera UI offers and then confirm the Set thumbnail updated."},{"type":"prose","html":"On 1.0.15 or later, a widget that shows this Set should pick up the new picture."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on a disposable duplicate, not on a Demo.","Choose the take-photo artwork command.","If the camera is blank, grant Camera access in iOS Settings and try again.","Take one still image and confirm the Set row shows it.","Return to the library and identify that Set from the new photo without reading the name."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the difference between camera artwork and recording a sample? <strong>Answer:</strong> Camera artwork is a thumbnail on the Set. Samples are audio recorded into Drum Rack or Sampler.","What should you check if the camera screen is blank? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS Camera permission for Note (fixed/called out in RN 1.0.15).","Does a camera thumbnail upload the Set to Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Upload is a separate Set Options command."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (blank camera screen when no rights were given)","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Take Photo)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Shoot one artwork photo for LAB-01 or for a throwaway duplicate. Confirm the library thumbnail matches the shot. Do not use the camera artwork as a substitute for backing up the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-6","title":"1.3.6 — Upload to Ableton Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.6 — Upload to Ableton Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start an upload from Set Options and know what “uploaded” means: the Set moves into the Cloud section and becomes available to Live and other signed-in devices."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> is a Set Options command. Tap the three dots next to the play button and choose it. Note needs an internet connection. If Wi-Fi and mobile data are both off, the upload starts automatically when a connection returns."},{"type":"prose","html":"After a successful upload the Set leaves the <strong>Local</strong> section and appears in the Ableton Cloud section of the library. In Live 11.2.5 or later, signed into the same ableton.com email, the Set shows under <strong>Cloud</strong> in Places as a folder you can open. Other Note installs on that account see it too. Cloud holds eight Sets total."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is transfer, not backup. The project also remains on the device under Files. Removing the Set from Cloud later can break sample references in a Live Set that still points at Cloud, which is why you Collect All and Save in Live after you open a Note Set. Do not upload every sketch. Upload the one you intend to open on the computer or on another device, then rotate slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can also reach Cloud from App Settings and from the library banner. Those entry points are the same service. This lesson is the Set Options path."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Cloud is enabled, or enable it from the toggle that appears when you choose <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>.","Duplicate LAB-01 if you do not want the working name occupying a slot. Use the duplicate as the upload test.","Open Set Options on that test Set and tap <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>.","When the transfer finishes, confirm the Set now sits in the Ableton Cloud section.","If you use Live on this account, look under <strong>Cloud</strong> in Places. If not, stop at the Note library confirmation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["After upload, where does the Set appear in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Ableton Cloud section of the Sets Library.","How many Sets can Cloud hold? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight.","Is upload a backup of the whole library? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud transfers up to eight Sets. The local file still lives in Files, and Share To… is the backup path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud with Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Upload one test Set you can spare a Cloud slot for. Confirm it moved to the Cloud section. Leave LAB-01-BACKUP Local so you still have an on-device copy that is not occupying Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-7","title":"1.3.7 — Share To… overview (deep in 1.5)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.7 — Share To… overview (deep in 1.5)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Share To…</strong> from Set Options and state what it produces, without finishing every destination yet."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Share To…</strong> is the Set Options command for handing a Set to another app, to another person, or to Files. The official sharing article and the manual agree on the result: Note creates an <code>.ablbundle</code> file (for example <code>MySong.ablbundle</code>). That file is similar to a Live Set and includes the Set, its samples, and metadata. It can be opened in another Note install or in Live 11.2.5 and later."},{"type":"prose","html":"The sharing article’s steps are: from the home screen, tap Set Options to the right of the Set name → <strong>Share To…</strong> → choose a sharing app (AirDrop, iMessage, WhatsApp, and so on). The manual adds that <strong>Share To…</strong> can also copy the Set or save it to the Files app."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the collaborator and backup path. Cloud cannot send a Set into someone else’s account. If you need a friend to open the sketch, you send the <code>.ablbundle</code>. If you need a copy that survives deleting Note, you save that bundle outside the app. Module 1.5 walks each destination. This lesson is only: find the command, know the file type, do not confuse it with <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud = signed-in transfer to your Live / Note / Move / Push. <strong>Share To…</strong> = a portable file."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on LAB-01 or on the BACKUP copy.","Tap <strong>Share To…</strong> and read the system share sheet. Do not send anything yet unless you are ready to save a bundle to Files.","Note which destinations you see (AirDrop, Messages, Files, Copy, and any others on this device).","Dismiss the sheet if you are not exporting now.","From memory, say what file type <strong>Share To…</strong> creates and name two places it can go."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What file does <strong>Share To…</strong> create? <strong>Answer:</strong> An <code>.ablbundle</code> that includes the Set, samples, and metadata.","Who can open that file? <strong>Answer:</strong> Another Note install, or Live 11.2.5 and later.","Why use <strong>Share To…</strong> instead of Cloud for a collaborator? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cloud is only for the same Ableton account. <strong>Share To…</strong> sends a file without sharing that login."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Sharing Note Sets (Set Options → Share To… → sharing app → <code>.ablbundle</code>)","Note FAQ, Can I share my sets to someone else’s Cloud?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Share To…</strong> once on LAB-01 and cancel or save to Files if you want an early backup. You will produce a kept <code>.ablbundle</code> as the Module 1.5 practice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-3-8","title":"1.3.8 — Delete a Set only after a backup exists","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.8 — Delete a Set only after a backup exists","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.3 — Set Options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat <strong>Delete</strong> as a permanent local removal, and only use it after a duplicate or an <code>.ablbundle</code> exists."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Options includes <strong>Delete</strong>. The secondary extract states it permanently removes the Set from your device. That is not “remove from Cloud but keep Local,” and it is not “move to Recently Deleted” in any sourced way. {type:'gap'} The official manual §3.3 does not describe Delete or a trash/undo window. Assume gone means gone."},{"type":"prose","html":"Before you delete anything you might need, make a copy you control:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Duplicate</strong> in Set Options keeps a second Set in the library.","<strong>Share To…</strong> writes an <code>.ablbundle</code> you can save to Files, AirDrop, or another app. The official sharing article says to use <strong>Share To…</strong> to share with a collaborator <em>or to back up your files</em>.","Before deleting the <em>app</em>, the FAQ says to back up the entire Ableton Note folder or individual Projects."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud does not qualify as that backup. Cloud holds eight Sets, is a transfer lane, and the local project is what you actually delete from the device. A Cloud-only mindset is how people erase the Files copy and then rotate the Cloud slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"Never delete LAB-01 until LAB-01-BACKUP exists and, better, an <code>.ablbundle</code> sits in Files. Never delete a Demo to “clean up” unless you accept you are removing factory listening content from this install."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong> is in the library.","Optionally run <strong>Share To…</strong> and save an <code>.ablbundle</code> to Files (Module 1.5).","Create a throwaway Set with <strong>+ New</strong>, play nothing important, and only delete <em>that</em> Set.","Open Set Options on the throwaway → <strong>Delete</strong>. Confirm it disappears from the library.","Confirm LAB-01 and LAB-01-BACKUP are still listed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Delete</strong> do according to the secondary extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> It permanently removes the Set from the device.","Name two sourced ways to keep a copy before deleting. <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Duplicate</strong>, and <strong>Share To…</strong> (an <code>.ablbundle</code>). Backing up the Ableton Note folder also works.","Does a Cloud upload replace those backups? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud is transfer, not a backup system."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Delete permanently removes the Set)","Sharing Note Sets (Share To… for collaborators or backup)","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note; Can Note projects synced to Cloud be deleted from my device?","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Delete only a throwaway Set. Keep LAB-01 and LAB-01-BACKUP. If you do not yet have a bundle in Files, make one before you ever delete a Set you care about."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-1-4-104","code":"1.4","title":"1.4","blurb":"1.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-4-1","title":"1.4.1 — Path: Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.1 — Path: Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the iOS Files app and walk to the Ableton Note folder so you know where Sets and related files actually live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note stores its files on the device, not only inside the app icon. The FAQ path is <strong>Device → Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong>. Use <strong>On My iPhone</strong> or <strong>On My iPad</strong>, matching the hardware in your hand. Do not look in iCloud Drive first. The official location is the on-device Ableton Note folder."},{"type":"prose","html":"That folder is why deleting the app is dangerous. The FAQ says to back up the entire Ableton Note folder, or individual Projects via <strong>Share To…</strong>, before you delete the application. If you never open Files, you have never seen the copy you would lose."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud-synced Sets still have a local store. The FAQ places those projects under <strong>Files → On My Device → Ableton Note → Projects</strong>. Uploading does not remove the Files copy. Treat Files as the on-device original and Cloud as the transfer lane."},{"type":"prose","html":"Inside Ableton Note you will also meet <strong>User Library</strong> paths for imported and recorded samples (Module 1.4.7). This lesson is only the top of the tree: find <strong>Ableton Note</strong> under <strong>On My iPhone/iPad</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Leave Note. Open the iOS <strong>Files</strong> app.","Under <strong>Browse</strong>, open <strong>On My iPhone</strong> or <strong>On My iPad</strong>.","Open the <strong>Ableton Note</strong> folder.","Look at the folder names you see. Do not delete anything.","Return to Note, then walk the same Files path again from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the official path to Note’s on-device files? <strong>Answer:</strong> Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note.","Should you look in iCloud Drive for the official Set store? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ path is On My iPhone/iPad.","Why open this folder before you ever delete the app? <strong>Answer:</strong> Because that folder is what you must back up first."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note","Note FAQ, Can Note projects synced to Ableton Cloud be deleted from my device?","Working with Samples in Note (Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Navigate to <strong>Ableton Note</strong> in Files and leave the app sitting on that folder for a few seconds so the path sticks. Confirm you can get there without this page."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-2","title":"1.4.2 — Projects folder meaning","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.2 — Projects folder meaning","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Identify the <strong>Projects</strong> folder as the local store for Note Sets, including Sets that are also in Cloud."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ is specific: Note projects synced to Ableton Cloud also remain stored locally under <strong>Files → On My Device → Ableton Note → Projects</strong>. <strong>Projects</strong> is the on-device home of your Sets. Cloud does not replace that folder. A Set can be listed in the Cloud section of the library and still sit in <strong>Projects</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the meaning of the folder. If you need to see whether a sketch still exists after a Cloud rotation, look in <strong>Projects</strong> before you assume it is gone. If you need to back up “the songs” rather than samples or presets, this is the folder the FAQ points at, together with <strong>Share To…</strong> for individual Projects."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not document the internal file names inside each Project (beyond Live-side <code>.abl</code> / <code>.ablbundle</code> after transfer) or a supported way to rename those folders in Files. Do not rename Project folders in Files as a substitute for Set Options → <strong>Rename</strong>. Do not delete folders in Files to “clean up” unless you accept the same risk as <strong>Delete</strong> in Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sample libraries are next door, not inside every Project in a way this lesson needs. <strong>User Library → Imported</strong> and <strong>User Library → Recordings</strong> hold imported and saved recordings. <strong>Projects</strong> holds the Sets."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong>.","Open <strong>Projects</strong>.","Scan the list and look for names that match LAB-01 and LAB-01-BACKUP.","Do not rename, move, or delete any folder.","Close Files, reopen the path from memory, and point at <strong>Projects</strong> without hesitation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do Cloud-synced Note projects remain on the device? <strong>Answer:</strong> Files → On My Device → Ableton Note → Projects.","If you remove a Set from Cloud, is <strong>Projects</strong> automatically empty? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud is not the only store. The FAQ says synced projects also remain local.","Should you rename a Project folder in Files? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not as a sourced workflow. Rename from Set Options in Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Can Note projects synced to Ableton Cloud be deleted from my device?","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note","Working with Samples in Note (sibling User Library paths)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Projects</strong> and confirm LAB-01 is visible there. Write “Projects = local Sets” on a card. That sentence is the whole folder."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-3","title":"1.4.3 — Auto-save every ~10–15 seconds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.3 — Auto-save every ~10–15 seconds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Work without hunting for a Save button, and know how often Note writes the Set in the background."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note auto-saves in the background every 10–15 seconds, depending on overall system activity. That FAQ sentence is the whole save model. There is no sourced “Save Set” command in Session View. Leaving a Set with the back arrow is safe because the auto-save has already been running."},{"type":"prose","html":"The interval is approximate. Heavy system activity can stretch it toward the long end of that range. Do not assume a change you made in the last second is already on disk. If you are about to force-quit Note, switch apps for a long time, or let the device die, wait a short moment after the last important edit so at least one auto-save cycle can finish."},{"type":"prose","html":"Auto-save writes the local Set. It is not an upload to Cloud and it is not an <code>.ablbundle</code> in Files. Those are explicit actions. Auto-save is why a crash is usually recoverable (next lesson) and why you can treat the back arrow as an ordinary exit."},{"type":"prose","html":"The course release-notes digest points at the same FAQ interval under 1.0.15 / 1.0.6. Use the FAQ wording, not a guessed number."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open LAB-01.","Make a small, obvious change (move a track, or play and leave the Set sitting after a later Capture lesson). For now, open Set Settings and nudge tempo one BPM, then nudge it back.","Watch the clock for about 15 seconds. Do not force-quit.","Tap the back arrow to the library, reopen the Set, and confirm the Set is intact.","Say the interval from memory: every 10–15 seconds, depending on system activity."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How often does Note auto-save? <strong>Answer:</strong> Every 10–15 seconds, depending on overall system activity.","Do you need to tap Save before using the back arrow? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Auto-save runs in the background.","Does auto-save upload the Set to Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Upload is a separate Cloud action."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note (“How often does Note save sets?”)","Note Release Notes digest (auto-save / crash behavior points at the FAQ)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change one harmless Set Setting on LAB-01, wait about 15 seconds, leave the Set, reopen it, and confirm the change stuck. Then put the setting back if you do not want it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-4","title":"1.4.4 — Crash resilience expectations","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.4 — Crash resilience expectations","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Know what Note claims it can recover after a crash, and what you still need to back up yourself."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ pairs two questions: how often Note saves, and whether a Set can be recovered if Note crashes. The answer is the auto-save: a background write every 10–15 seconds, depending on system activity, “to help prevent data loss in case of a crash.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the expectation. Work you finished more than one auto-save cycle ago should still be on disk. Work you did in the last few seconds might not. A crash is not a reason to contact Support. The FAQ says that if Note crashes, a crash report is sent automatically to Ableton’s developers and you do not need to write in."},{"type":"prose","html":"Crash resilience is not a backup strategy. Auto-save cannot help if you delete the Set, delete the app without copying the Ableton Note folder, or rely on Cloud as the only copy and then rotate that slot away. Resilience means “the app tried to write recently.” Backup means a duplicate, an <code>.ablbundle</code>, or a device backup you control."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not describe a crash-recovery dialog, a versions browser, or a way to restore an older auto-save. If the Set opens and the last idea is missing, rebuild it. Do not expect a Time Machine inside Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read the FAQ sentence about auto-save and crash prevention once, then close this page.","Write it from memory: interval, what it depends on, and why it exists.","Confirm LAB-01-BACKUP still exists so a crash is not your only safety net.","If Note ever does crash on you, reopen the Set and check the last change. Do not file a Support ticket just because of the crash.","If you find a <em>reproducible</em> bug, that is a different FAQ path: list the steps and submit them while logged into your Ableton account."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does auto-save exist to prevent? <strong>Answer:</strong> Data loss in case of a crash.","Should you contact Support after a one-off crash? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. A crash report is sent automatically.","Does a crash-recovery promise replace Duplicate or Share To…? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Auto-save is recent-write protection, not a backup system."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note","Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (“Note crashed. What should I do?”)","Note FAQ, I found a bug"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write two columns: “Recoverable after a crash” (edits older than ~15 seconds, Set still on disk) and “Not covered” (deleted Sets, deleted app, Cloud slot rotated away, last two seconds of work). Keep the BACKUP Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-5","title":"1.4.5 — Before deleting the app: backup entire Ableton Note folder","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.5 — Before deleting the app: backup entire Ableton Note folder","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Copy the entire Ableton Note folder, or individual Projects via <strong>Share To…</strong>, before you delete the Note app."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Deleting the Note application can take the on-device library with it. The FAQ is blunt: Note Sets and related files live in <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong>. Before you delete the app, back up that entire folder, or back up individual Projects with <strong>Share To…</strong>, or back up the iOS device itself."},{"type":"prose","html":"“Entire folder” matters. <strong>Projects</strong> holds Sets. <strong>User Library</strong> holds imported and recorded samples and, after 1.0.12, the migrated user-sample location. If you only AirDrop one <code>.ablbundle</code>, you keep that Set. You do not keep every other sketch, every imported sample, or every user preset. If you are leaving the app, copy the whole <strong>Ableton Note</strong> directory out of <strong>On My iPhone/iPad</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not give a click-by-click Files share recipe (Compress, AirDrop the folder, copy to a Mac). Use the iOS Files share sheet on the <strong>Ableton Note</strong> folder, or run a full device backup. Do not invent a hidden Note export-all command. There is none in the sources."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you open a Note Set in Live after you get off the phone, use <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> so Live has its own local copies. That protects the computer side. It does not replace backing up the phone folder before you delete Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong>.","Long-press the <strong>Ableton Note</strong> folder and look at the share/copy options your iOS version offers. Do not delete the folder.","If you are actually uninstalling Note, send that folder somewhere you control, or run an iOS backup, <em>before</em> deleting the app.","Also <strong>Share To…</strong> LAB-01 as a second, smaller safety copy.","Only after those copies exist would you delete the app. For this course, do <strong>not</strong> delete Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What must you back up before deleting the Note app? <strong>Answer:</strong> The entire Ableton Note folder, or individual Projects via <strong>Share To…</strong>, or the iOS device.","Why is one <code>.ablbundle</code> not enough if you are leaving the app? <strong>Answer:</strong> It keeps that Set only, not the rest of Projects and User Library.","Does <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> in Live replace the phone backup? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It protects the Live Set on the computer after you open a Note Set there."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note","Sharing Note Sets (Share To… to back up files)","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (Collect All and Save after opening in Live)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Walk to the Ableton Note folder and identify it as the thing you would copy. Produce or confirm one <code>.ablbundle</code> of LAB-01 as a second line of defense. Do not uninstall Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-6","title":"1.4.6 — Cloud is not a backup system","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.6 — Cloud is not a backup system","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State Ableton’s own purpose for Cloud — share and sync across devices — and refuse to treat eight Cloud slots as an archive."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ asks whether Cloud-synced projects can be deleted from the device, then answers with the storage model: those projects also remain locally under <strong>Files → On My Device → Ableton Note → Projects</strong>. Then the purpose sentence: Cloud is designed as a way to share and sync Note Sets across devices, <strong>not as a file backup or storage system</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the rule for the rest of the course. Cloud moves a Set to Live, to another Note install, to Move, or to Push on the same account. It holds eight Sets. When you need a ninth, you rotate. Rotation is expected. Archives are not."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you delete the local Set, or delete the app, because “it’s in Cloud,” you have inverted the design. The local <strong>Projects</strong> folder is the copy that stays. The <code>.ablbundle</code> you save with <strong>Share To…</strong> is the copy you keep outside the app. Cloud is the pipe you turn on when you want that sketch on the computer today."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live-side risk matches the same idea. If you open a Note Set from Cloud and then remove it from Cloud, Live can lose samples unless you already used <strong>Collect All and Save</strong>. Cloud is a live transfer folder, not a vault."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write the FAQ purpose sentence from this page, then hide the page and write it again.","Count how many Sets you currently have in the Cloud section (0–8).","Point at LAB-01 in <strong>Projects</strong> in the Files app. That is the backup-relevant copy.","Point at <strong>Share To…</strong> on that Set. That is the off-device backup path.","Say one sentence you will not use: “I don’t need a duplicate, it’s in Cloud.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is Ableton Cloud for, in the FAQ’s words? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sharing and syncing Note Sets across devices, not file backup or storage.","If a Set is in Cloud, where is it also stored? <strong>Answer:</strong> Locally, under Files → On My Device → Ableton Note → Projects.","What do you use when you actually want a backup file? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Share To…</strong> (<code>.ablbundle</code>), a copy of the Ableton Note folder, or an iOS backup."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Can Note projects synced to Ableton Cloud be deleted from my device?","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note","Sharing Note Sets (Share To… to back up files)","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (missing files after removing a Set from Cloud)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Look at your Cloud section. If LAB-01 is there, confirm it is also in <strong>Projects</strong>. If it is only Local, leave it Local and still make an <code>.ablbundle</code>. Cloud is optional. Backup is not."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-4-7","title":"1.4.7 — User Library migration notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.7 — User Library migration notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.4 — On-device files and auto-save</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find today’s User Library paths and know that Note 1.0.12 migrated <strong>User Samples</strong> into <strong>User Library</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.12 migrated the <strong>User Samples</strong> folder to <strong>User Library</strong> in the Files app. The same release moved Recorded and Imported so they sit in <strong>User Library</strong> next to the Core Library inside the browser. If you used Note before 1.0.12 and something “vanished” after an update, look in <strong>User Library</strong> before you rebuild anything."},{"type":"prose","html":"Current on-device sample paths from the samples article:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library → Imported</strong>","<strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library → Recordings</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Inside Note’s browser the same places appear as <strong>Libraries → User Library → Imported</strong> and <strong>Libraries → User Library → Recordings</strong> (with <strong>Drum Sampler</strong> / <strong>Melodic Sampler</strong> subfolders for saved recordings). Unsaved recordings from the open Set live under <strong>Libraries → Current Set → Recordings</strong> until you save them."},{"type":"prose","html":"User presets you store from a modified Track Preset also land in the User Library, organized by instrument category. That is a different pile from <strong>Projects</strong>. <strong>Projects</strong> = Sets. <strong>User Library</strong> = your samples and saved presets."},{"type":"prose","html":"On the Live side, Cloud-synced audio samples are stored in Live’s User Library. If that Live User Library lives on a disconnected external drive, opening a Note Set from Cloud can show missing files. Reconnect the drive, relaunch Live. That is a Live-library problem, not a reason to delete Note’s folder."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong>.","Open <strong>User Library</strong> if it is there. Look for <strong>Imported</strong> and <strong>Recordings</strong>.","Open <strong>Projects</strong> once so you can feel the difference between the two folders.","In Note, open a Sampler or Drum Rack browser and walk <strong>Libraries → User Library</strong>.","From memory, name the 1.0.12 migration (User Samples → User Library) and the two sample subfolders."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 1.0.12 migrate? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>User Samples</strong> folder to <strong>User Library</strong> in the Files app.","Where are imported samples on the device now? <strong>Answer:</strong> Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library → Imported.","If Live reports missing Note samples and your Live User Library is on an external drive, what do you do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Quit Live, reconnect that drive, relaunch Live."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (User Samples → User Library; Recorded/Imported in User Library)","Working with Samples in Note, Sample storage locations","Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (User Library location moved or removed)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Navigate to <strong>Ableton Note</strong> in Files and confirm both <strong>Projects</strong> and <strong>User Library</strong> (if present on your install). Write one line: Sets go in Projects; samples and user presets go in User Library."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-1-5-105","code":"1.5","title":"1.5","blurb":"1.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-5-1","title":"1.5.1 — Share To… destinations","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.1 — Share To… destinations","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the <strong>Share To…</strong> sheet and name the destination types the sources actually list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From the Sets Library, tap Set Options (three dots next to the play button, to the right of the Set name) and tap <strong>Share To…</strong>. iOS then shows the system share sheet. Official destinations named in the extracts:"},{"type":"list","items":["Sharing apps, with examples <strong>AirDrop</strong>, <strong>iMessage</strong>, and <strong>WhatsApp</strong>","<strong>Save</strong> to the <strong>Files</strong> app","<strong>Copy</strong> the Set (manual wording)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Every destination receives the same payload: an <code>.ablbundle</code> that other Note installs and Live 11.2.5+ can open. The sheet is how you pick the pipe. It is not a second export format."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this menu when Cloud is the wrong tool: a collaborator on a different Ableton account, a backup you want on a Mac, or a copy in Files before you delete anything. Cloud remains the signed-in transfer to <em>your</em> Live, Note, Move, or Push."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not list every icon that can appear on an iOS share sheet (Mail, Drive, and so on). If an app accepts a file, you can try it. Do not claim official support beyond AirDrop, Messages/iMessage, WhatsApp, Files, and Copy."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a Mac, sharing the same Set into one folder many times can break the filename. That is a later lesson. For now, pick one destination and one folder."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on LAB-01.","Tap <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Read the sheet and write down the destinations you actually see.","Identify AirDrop, Files, and Copy if they are present. Note any extras.","Dismiss the sheet, then reopen it from memory and point at two destinations you would use for backup vs a friend."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you start <strong>Share To…</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sets Library → Set Options (three dots) → <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Name three destinations the official articles mention. <strong>Answer:</strong> AirDrop, iMessage, WhatsApp. The manual also names Files and Copy.","What file do all of those destinations send? <strong>Answer:</strong> An <code>.ablbundle</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Sharing Note Sets","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Note FAQ, Can I transfer Sets without using Ableton Cloud?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Share To…</strong> on LAB-01 and leave the sheet open long enough to list destinations. Next lessons send the file. The Module 1.5 practice is still: produce one kept <code>.ablbundle</code> of LAB-01."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-2","title":"1.5.2 — An .ablbundle contains the Set, its samples, and metadata","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.2 — An .ablbundle contains the Set, its samples, and metadata","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Explain what an <code>.ablbundle</code> holds so you know why it is the file you send, save, and open."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sharing a Set creates an <code>.ablbundle</code> file, for example <code>MySong.ablbundle</code>. The manual says this file is similar to a Live Set and includes all samples and metadata. The sharing article says the Set is sent as an <code>.ablbundle</code> that can be opened in Note or Live. A secondary extract adds instruments, MIDI data, and automation to that picture. Teach the official trio: the Set, its samples, and metadata."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is why <strong>Share To…</strong> is a real backup and a real collaboration format. A bounced WAV is only audio. An <code>.ablbundle</code> still has the clips, the devices, and the samples you recorded or imported into that Set. Another Note install can open it and keep working. Live 11.2.5 or later can open it as a Live Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"After Cloud upload, Live’s Cloud folder also contains a project-like folder with samples, recordings, a <code>.abl</code> file, and an <code>.ablbundle</code>. Double-clicking the <code>.ablbundle</code> in Live opens the Note project as a Live Set. <strong>Share To…</strong> is how you create that same kind of file without using Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the extension visible when you move the file on a computer. A later troubleshooting lesson covers macOS renaming that strips <code>.ablbundle</code> or appends a number after the extension. If the extension is gone, Live cannot open it as a bundle."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Run <strong>Share To…</strong> on LAB-01 and save the file to Files (or AirDrop it to a folder you can inspect).","Find the file and read the full name, including <code>.ablbundle</code>.","Confirm the name matches the Set (or the example pattern <code>Name.ablbundle</code>).","Do not rename the extension.","From memory, list the three official contents: Set, samples, metadata."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does an <code>.ablbundle</code> include according to the manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Set plus all samples and metadata (similar to a Live Set).","Who can open it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Other instances of Note, and Live 11.2.5 and later.","Why not send a WAV when you want someone to continue the sketch? <strong>Answer:</strong> A WAV is audio only. The bundle still has the playable Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (<code>.ablbundle</code> in Live’s Cloud folder)","Sharing Note Sets","Ableton Note.md §5.2 (instruments, MIDI, automation, samples)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Produce one <code>.ablbundle</code> of LAB-01 and leave it in Files. That file is the Module 1.5 deliverable. Confirm the extension is still <code>.ablbundle</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-3","title":"1.5.3 — AirDrop to iOS / Mac","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.3 — AirDrop to iOS / Mac","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Send an <code>.ablbundle</code> with AirDrop to another iPhone, iPad, or Mac without using Cloud."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"AirDrop is a named <strong>Share To…</strong> destination in both the manual and the sharing article. You stay on the Sets Library, open Set Options, tap <strong>Share To…</strong>, and choose <strong>AirDrop</strong>. Note hands the other device an <code>.ablbundle</code>. No Ableton account is shared. No Cloud slot is used."},{"type":"prose","html":"On the receiving iPhone or iPad, the sharing article’s open path is: select the <code>.ablbundle</code> → tap the <strong>open with</strong> icon (bottom left) → choose <strong>Ableton Note</strong>. The Set opens in Note. On a Mac, the same file can be saved to a folder and opened in Live 11.2.5+ or copied onward."},{"type":"prose","html":"AirDrop is also how you import <em>samples</em> into Note from another device. That is a different file (WAV, AIFF, M4A, MP3) and a different accept/import dialog. This lesson is Sets only. Do not drop a bundle and expect it to land in a Drum Rack pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you AirDrop the same Set into the same Mac folder many times, macOS can damage the filename (number after the extension, or a missing <code>.ablbundle</code>). Use a new folder or fix the name, as the troubleshooting article describes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Share To…</strong> on LAB-01.","Choose <strong>AirDrop</strong>.","Send the bundle to a Mac you own, or to a second iOS device with Note, if you have one. If you have neither, save to Files instead and treat this as a dry run of the share sheet.","On the receiver, confirm the file is named <code>*.ablbundle</code>.","If the receiver is iOS with Note, open the bundle in Note using <strong>Open with</strong>. If it is a Mac, leave the file in a clearly named folder for Lesson 1.5.6."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does AirDrop use one of your eight Cloud slots? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It sends a file.","How do you open a received bundle in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select the <code>.ablbundle</code>, tap <strong>Open with</strong>, choose Ableton Note.","What macOS problem should you watch for if you send the same Set repeatedly to one folder? <strong>Answer:</strong> A number appended after the extension, or a missing <code>.ablbundle</code> extension."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Sharing Note Sets","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (Share To… / AirDrop name damage)","Working with Samples in Note (AirDrop of audio is a different workflow)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"AirDrop LAB-01 to a Mac or second iOS device if you can. Otherwise save the <code>.ablbundle</code> to Files. Either way you now have a copy outside Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-4","title":"1.5.4 — Save to Files","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.4 — Save to Files","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save an <code>.ablbundle</code> into the Files app so you have a backup that does not depend on Cloud or on Note remaining installed."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual lists saving to the Files app as one of the <strong>Share To…</strong> outcomes. That is the simplest backup you can make without another device. The share sheet writes <code>YourName.ablbundle</code> wherever you point Files: On My iPhone/iPad, a folder you created, or another location Files can see."},{"type":"prose","html":"This copy is not the live <strong>Projects</strong> folder. <strong>Projects</strong> is Note’s working store. A bundle you save is a snapshot. If you keep writing in Note after you save, the bundle does not update until you <strong>Share To…</strong> again. Date the file or put it in a folder named for the day if you need to tell snapshots apart. {type:'gap'} Note does not document automatic bundle versioning."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not save over the <strong>Ableton Note → Projects</strong> directory as a clever trick. Save <em>next to</em> your other backups, or in a course folder you create in Files. If you later delete the Note app, a bundle that lived only inside Ableton Note’s folder can disappear with it. A bundle saved in a folder you own is the one that survives."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a computer, keep the <code>.ablbundle</code> extension intact."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on LAB-01 → <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Choose the Files destination (Save to Files / Save to Files app).","Pick a folder that is <strong>not</strong> Ableton Note → Projects. Create a folder such as <strong>NOTE-COURSE-BACKUPS</strong> if you need one.","Save, then open Files and confirm <code>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01.ablbundle</code> (or the current Set name) is there.","Open the share sheet one more time from memory and hit Files without looking at this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What file does Save to Files write? <strong>Answer:</strong> An <code>.ablbundle</code> snapshot of the Set.","Does that file keep updating as you edit the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Share again to make a new snapshot.","Why avoid saving your only backup inside Ableton Note → Projects? <strong>Answer:</strong> Deleting the app can take that folder with it. Keep a copy in a folder you own."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Sharing Note Sets","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note (back up via Share To… before deleting the app)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save LAB-01 to a <strong>NOTE-COURSE-BACKUPS</strong> folder in Files. That kept bundle is the Module 1.5 practice object. Confirm it still has the <code>.ablbundle</code> extension."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-5","title":"1.5.5 — Open bundle in another Note install","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.5 — Open bundle in another Note install","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open a received <code>.ablbundle</code> in Ableton Note on a second iPhone or iPad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Shared <code>.ablbundle</code> files can be opened in other instances of Note. That is how you move a sketch to a second device without Cloud, and how a collaborator works in their own copy of the app."},{"type":"prose","html":"The official open path from the sharing article:"},{"type":"list","items":["Select the <code>.ablbundle</code> file.","Tap the <strong>open with</strong> icon in the bottom left corner.","Select <strong>Ableton Note</strong>. The Set opens in Note."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"The receiving install must be Note on iOS/iPadOS 15.0 or later. There is no Android Note. The person opening the file does not need your Ableton Cloud login. They need the app and the file."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once opened, the Set becomes a local Set on that device. Edits they make are theirs. Nothing writes back to your Cloud or to your original <strong>Projects</strong> folder. If you want their changes, they <strong>Share To…</strong> a new bundle back to you. Cloud is one-way in a different sense (Note → Live, not Live → Note); collaboration via bundle is also file-based and one shot at a time."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you only have one device, open the bundle you saved to Files on <em>this</em> install to confirm Note accepts it. You may get a second library row. That is enough to prove the format."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Locate the LAB-01 <code>.ablbundle</code> you saved or AirDropped.","Select the file.","Tap <strong>Open with</strong> and choose <strong>Ableton Note</strong>.","Confirm the Set opens in Session View and the tracks you expect are there.","Return to the library and note whether a new row appeared. Do not delete LAB-01."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the three official steps to open a bundle in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select the <code>.ablbundle</code>, tap <strong>Open with</strong>, choose Ableton Note.","Does the receiver need your Cloud account? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They need Note and the file.","Do their later edits sync back to you automatically? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They must send a new <code>.ablbundle</code> (or you use Cloud only on <em>your</em> devices)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Sharing Note Sets (open with Ableton Note)","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Note FAQ, Can I transfer Note Sets from one iOS device to another?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open your LAB-01 bundle in Note on this device or a second device. Play one scene (or preview from the library) to confirm the Set arrived intact."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-6","title":"1.5.6 — Open bundle in Live 11.2.5+","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.6 — Open bundle in Live 11.2.5+","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open an <code>.ablbundle</code> in Ableton Live 11.2.5 or later and Collect All and Save so the Live Set no longer depends on Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note Sets open in every Live edition — Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, Education — starting at Live <strong>11.2.5</strong>. An <code>.ablbundle</code> from <strong>Share To…</strong> is a valid way to get there without Cloud. On the computer, double-click the <code>.ablbundle</code> (or open it from Live). The manual describes the same file opening as a Live Set when it arrives via Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"Immediately use <strong>File → Collect All and Save</strong>. That copies referenced samples into the Live project folder. If you skip it, later missing-file errors are on you, especially if the Note Set later leaves Cloud or the phone is not around."},{"type":"prose","html":"Two computer-side traps from the troubleshooting article: if you <strong>Share To…</strong> the same Set into the same folder repeatedly, macOS may append a number <em>after</em> the extension, or increment a trailing number and drop <code>.ablbundle</code>. Either rename (remove the extra number, or restore <code>.ablbundle</code>) or share into a different folder. Then open again."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live Sets cannot be sent back into Note. Once you save as a Live Set, that modified version stays in Live. The bundle you opened is the hand-off, not a round trip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Put LAB-01’s <code>.ablbundle</code> on the computer (AirDrop, Files, cable). Confirm the name ends in <code>.ablbundle</code> with nothing after it.","Confirm Live is 11.2.5 or later (Live → About Live).","Double-click the bundle or open it from Live.","When the Set appears, choose <strong>File → Collect All and Save</strong>.","Play the Set in Live and confirm samples are present. If a file will not open, fix the filename using the troubleshooting rules, then retry."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the minimum Live version for a Note bundle? <strong>Answer:</strong> 11.2.5.","What must you do the first time you open a Note Set in Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Collect All and Save.","Can you send the resulting Live Set back into Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Transfer is Note → Live only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3; §10.1","Note FAQ, What Live licenses work with Ableton Note?; Collect All and Save","Note Compatibility (Live 11.2.5+; all editions)","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (Share To… name damage)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have Live 11.2.5+, open the LAB-01 bundle and Collect All and Save. If you do not have Live yet, claim Lite from Note Settings later and stop after confirming the bundle is ready on disk."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-7","title":"1.5.7 — Collaborators without sharing Cloud login","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.7 — Collaborators without sharing Cloud login","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Send a Set to another person without giving them your Ableton account, using <strong>Share To…</strong> instead of Cloud."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud is account-locked. The FAQ says Live Sets synced to Ableton Cloud can only be accessed by the person signed in with the same account on both Note and Live. You cannot drop a Set into someone else’s Cloud. If you need to share with collaborators, use <strong>Share To…</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the whole collaboration policy. You export an <code>.ablbundle</code>. They open it in Note or in Live 11.2.5+. They work in their own library. If they send changes back, they export a new bundle. There is no shared Cloud folder, no simultaneous edit, and no reason to give away your ableton.com password."},{"type":"prose","html":"The sharing article names AirDrop, iMessage, and WhatsApp as example pipes. Any of those is acceptable if the other person can receive a file. Tell them the open steps: select the <code>.ablbundle</code> → <strong>Open with</strong> → Ableton Note. If they work in Live, tell them 11.2.5+ and <strong>Collect All and Save</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse this with Link. Link keeps devices in time on a network. It does not transmit a Set. Do not confuse it with uploading to Cloud “so they can see it.” They cannot, unless they are you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Decide one collaborator path you actually have: AirDrop, Messages, or Files → another app.","<strong>Share To…</strong> LAB-01 (or a duplicate stripped of anything you do not want to send) through that path.","If you have no collaborator today, send the bundle to yourself on a second device or to a Mac.","Write the two rules you will tell them: same account is not required; they open the <code>.ablbundle</code>.","Confirm you did <strong>not</strong> share your Ableton password or disable Cloud to “let them in.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you put a Set in someone else’s Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud is the same Ableton account only.","What do you send a collaborator instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> An <code>.ablbundle</code> via <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Does Link replace <strong>Share To…</strong> for sending a Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Link syncs tempo/time. It does not transfer the Set file."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Can I share my sets to someone else’s Cloud?","Sharing Note Sets","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note Manual §2.2.1 Link (separate feature)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Produce the collaborator file: the same LAB-01 <code>.ablbundle</code> already in your backups folder. Write one sentence you would send with it: “Open this in Note or Live 11.2.5+, then Collect All and Save if you use Live.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-5-8","title":"1.5.8 — Copy Set option","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.8 — Copy Set option","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.5 — Share To… and <code>.ablbundle</code></em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the copy action the manual places inside <strong>Share To…</strong>, and keep it distinct from <strong>Duplicate</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §3.3 says <strong>Share To…</strong> allows you to share your Set to AirDrop or other apps, <strong>copy the Set</strong>, or save it to the Files app. Copy is therefore a documented sibling of AirDrop and Save to Files, not a separate Set Options row like <strong>Duplicate</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Duplicate</strong> (Lesson 1.3.2) makes another Set row in your library. Copy, as named in the share path, is the share-sheet way to put the Set on the clipboard / copy the file so another app or location can receive it. Both exist. They are not the same control."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not print the exact share-sheet label (<strong>Copy</strong>, <strong>Copy Set</strong>, <strong>Copy to…</strong>) or say whether the clipboard holds the <code>.ablbundle</code> or creates a second library Set. On your iOS version, open <strong>Share To…</strong> and read the real wording. If you need another <em>Set in the library</em>, use <strong>Duplicate</strong>. If you need a <em>file</em> to paste or hand off, use the copy item on the share sheet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not use Copy as Cloud. Do not use it as the only backup if you cannot later find where the copied file landed. Save to Files is the clearer backup because you choose the folder."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Set Options on LAB-01 → <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Find the copy-related item on the sheet. Write the exact label you see.","If you invoke it, immediately paste or check the destination so you know what iOS did. If you cannot find the result, save to Files instead.","Open Set Options again and look at <strong>Duplicate</strong>. Say one sentence that separates the two.","Confirm LAB-01 and LAB-01-BACKUP still exist as library rows."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does the manual place “copy the Set”? <strong>Answer:</strong> Among the <strong>Share To…</strong> options, with AirDrop/other apps and save to Files.","What do you tap when you want a second row in the Sets Library? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Options → <strong>Duplicate</strong>.","Is the on-screen copy label documented word-for-word? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Read the share sheet on your device."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Sharing Note Sets","Ableton Note.md §2.1 (Duplicate vs Share To…)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Share To…</strong>, write the exact copy label, then save LAB-01 to Files if you still need a folder you can point at. Library branching stays on <strong>Duplicate</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-5-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-1-6-106","code":"1.6","title":"1.6","blurb":"1.6","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l1-6-1","title":"1.6.1 — Cloud is for transfer, not long-term backup","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.1 — Cloud is for transfer, not long-term backup","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Describe Ableton Cloud as a one-way transfer service with eight slots, not as an archive of every Set you ever make."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton Cloud stores up to eight Sets from Note and Move and lets you access them across Ableton products. The transfer chapter says you use it to share Note Sets with Live, Move, Push, or other devices that have Note installed. The FAQ purpose line is the same idea in fewer words: easy transfer of Note Sets from iOS into Live’s browser, plus availability on other Note installs with Cloud enabled."},{"type":"prose","html":"Direction is one way. Sets from Note open in Live. Live Sets do not open in Note. Once you import a Note Set into Live and save it as a Live Set, that modified version cannot come back. Plan the hand-off as a finish-on-the-computer step, not a ping-pong."},{"type":"prose","html":"Because capacity is eight, Cloud is a <em>current</em> transfer tray. You upload the Set you need on the computer this week. You remove it from Cloud when you are done — after Live has <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> — and you upload the next one. The local Set remains in <strong>Projects</strong>. The long-term copies are <strong>Share To…</strong> bundles, the Ableton Note folder, or a device backup."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you treat Cloud as Dropbox for every sketch, you will hit the cap, rotate the wrong Set, and then discover Live is missing samples. Transfer, then collect, then free the slot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write the transfer list from memory: Live, Move, Push, other Note installs on the same account.","Write the backup list from memory: <strong>Share To…</strong> <code>.ablbundle</code>, Ableton Note folder, iOS backup, <strong>Projects</strong>.","Open the Sets Library and count Cloud rows. Those are slots in use, not an archive.","Confirm LAB-01 still exists in <strong>Projects</strong> whether or not it is in Cloud.","Say the one-way rule: Note → Live, never Live → Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is Cloud for? <strong>Answer:</strong> Transfer and sync of up to eight Note/Move Sets across Ableton products, especially into Live’s browser.","Is Cloud a backup system? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ says it is not a file backup or storage system.","Which direction does sharing between Note and Live work? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note to Live only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, What is Ableton Cloud and what is it for?","Note FAQ, Can Note projects synced to Cloud be deleted from my device?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Upload a test Set only if you have a free Cloud slot and a reason to open it in Live. Otherwise leave Cloud empty and keep the LAB-01 <code>.ablbundle</code>. The point is the distinction, not filling all eight slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-2","title":"1.6.2 — Enable toggle; browser login flow","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.2 — Enable toggle; browser login flow","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn Ableton Cloud on in Note and complete the browser login that connects the app to your ableton.com account."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable Cloud from the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle in App Settings. The same toggle also appears in the <strong>Enable Ableton Cloud</strong> area of the Set Library and via <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> in Set Options. You do not need three accounts. You need one successful enable."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turning the toggle on opens ableton.com in a web browser. Follow the prompts to connect the service to your Ableton user account. An ableton.com account is required for Cloud and not required to use Note itself. If the browser jumps straight to an “Enable Ableton Cloud” page, you are already logged into <em>some</em> account. Confirm it is the right one: tap <strong>Go to Ableton.com</strong>, log out from the menu, return to Note, enable the toggle again, and log in with the email that owns your Live license."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you grant access, return to Note with the <strong>Open</strong> button the browser shows. Back in the library you should see the Cloud area. Sets already in Cloud (for example from Move) appear there."},{"type":"prose","html":"If enable fails, check internet, then the Cloud server status (Note 1.1.2 added a Status page link in settings). Do not keep toggling on airplane mode and calling it a broken account."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open App Settings from the three dots at the bottom-left of the Sets Library.","Turn <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> on. Complete the browser prompts.","If the page skips login, log out of ableton.com in that browser and enable the toggle again.","Sign in with the email you use for Live. Grant access. Tap <strong>Open</strong> to return to Note.","Confirm the Cloud area is visible in the Sets Library."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where can you turn Cloud on in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> App Settings toggle, the library’s Enable Ableton Cloud area, or Upload to Ableton Cloud in Set Options.","What does enabling the toggle open? <strong>Answer:</strong> ableton.com in a web browser, to connect Cloud to your Ableton account.","Do you need that account to sketch in Note without Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (account required to authorize Cloud)","Note FAQ, How do I enable Ableton Cloud in Note?","Note Sets in Ableton Cloud not Appearing in Live, Re-Enabling Cloud in Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable Cloud once with the correct email. If Cloud is already on, toggle it only if you need to re-auth. Then stop. Uploading is the next lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-3","title":"1.6.3 — Use the same Ableton account email as the Live license","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.3 — Use the same Ableton account email as the Live license","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sign into Cloud on Note and on Live with the single email that owns your Live license so uploaded Sets can appear in Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The most common Cloud failure is two emails. The troubleshooting article: the Ableton account used to enable Cloud in Note must match the account used to enable Cloud in Live, or synced Sets will not appear. Log in using the same email address. The FAQ adds: when you enable Cloud in Note, sign in to Ableton.com using the email associated with your Ableton Live license."},{"type":"prose","html":"That pair is the rule. Note Cloud email = Live Cloud email = Live license email. A personal Gmail on the phone and a school address on the computer will look like “Cloud is broken.” It is not. It is two libraries."},{"type":"prose","html":"Re-enable on both sides if you already mixed them. In Note: toggle <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> off and on, log out of ableton.com in the mobile browser via <strong>Go to Ableton.com</strong> → Menu → Log out, enable again, sign in with the Live email, tap <strong>Open</strong>. In Live: right-click the Cloud label and <strong>Sign Out</strong>, select Cloud in Places, <strong>Sign In</strong>, log out of the desktop browser if it is the wrong account, sign in with the same email, then <strong>Return to Application</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can use Note without any account. The moment you want Live to see the Set, the emails must match."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write the email you use to authorize Live (the license email).","In the mobile browser used by Note’s Cloud toggle, confirm you are on that same email. Log out and back in if not.","In Live, open Cloud in Places and confirm the signed-in identity is that same email.","If they differ, run the re-enable steps on both products before you upload again.","Recite the match rule without this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which email must Note’s Cloud login use? <strong>Answer:</strong> The email associated with your Ableton Live license — the same email Live’s Cloud uses.","What happens if Note and Live use different emails? <strong>Answer:</strong> Synced Sets do not appear in Live.","Where do you sign out of Cloud in Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Right-click the Cloud label in Live’s browser and choose Sign Out."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, My sets in Ableton Cloud aren’t showing up in Live","Note Sets in Ableton Cloud not Appearing in Live, Steps 2–3","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (same registered email address)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot or write the email you will use on both sides. Do not upload a test Set until those two logins match."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-4","title":"1.6.4 — Cloud holds eight Sets; rotate which Sets occupy the slots","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.4 — Cloud holds eight Sets; rotate which Sets occupy the slots","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat Cloud as eight transferable slots and rotate Sets in and out instead of trying to store a whole catalog there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can sync a total of eight Sets. The manual calls Cloud a service that stores up to eight Sets from Note and Move. Note 1.2 raised that cap from five to eight. If your memory is “Cloud holds five,” you are on a pre-1.2 fact."},{"type":"prose","html":"Eight is a hard working set, not a library. Move Sets already in Cloud count against the same eight. A Demo is not your transfer object. Your Local catalog can be much larger; only the eight you upload occupy Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"Rotation is the professional habit. Upload the Set you will open in Live today. In Live, <strong>Collect All and Save</strong>. Then remove that Set from Cloud when you need the slot, and upload the next one. The local copy stays in <strong>Projects</strong>. Removing from Cloud while Live still references Cloud samples is the missing-file case in the troubleshooting article — collect first, remove second."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts describe upload clearly; they do not spell out a labeled “Remove from Cloud” control word-for-word. On your version, use Set Options on a Cloud Set and look for the command that takes it out of Cloud. Do not delete the local Set when you only meant to free a slot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Count Sets in the Ableton Cloud section. Write <em>n</em> / 8.","List which of those eight you still need on the computer this week.","If you are at 8 and you need to upload LAB-01, choose a Set you have already collected in Live (or never opened in Live) to remove from Cloud.","Upload only after a slot is free.","Confirm the local original is still in <strong>Projects</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many Sets can you sync to Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight (five before Note 1.2).","Do Move Sets in Cloud count toward that eight? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Cloud stores up to eight Sets from Note and Move together.","What must you do in Live before you remove a Set from Cloud if you already opened it there? <strong>Answer:</strong> Collect All and Save so samples stay in the Live project folder."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, How many sets can I upload to Ableton Cloud?","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Cloud now supports 8 sets; was 5)","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (missing files after removing from Cloud)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write your current Cloud occupancy as a fraction out of eight. Plan which one Set, if any, you will upload for Gate A. Do not fill unused slots for sport."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-5","title":"1.6.5 — Upload entry points (Settings, library banner, Set Options)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.5 — Upload entry points (Settings, library banner, Set Options)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Reach Cloud from any of the three official Note entry points and know that they are one service."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note exposes Cloud in three places on purpose so you can enable or upload without hunting:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>App Settings</strong> — the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle. This is the FAQ’s “from the Settings page, turn on Ableton Cloud.”","<strong>Enable Ableton Cloud</strong> in the Set Library — a banner / area on the home screen. Same toggle.","<strong>Set Options → Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> — the transfer command on a specific Set. The transfer chapter’s pictured path is: three dots next to the play button → <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Settings and the library banner are how you <em>authorize</em> the service. Set Options is how you <em>send a Set</em>. The first time you tap Upload without Cloud on, Note can throw you through the same browser login. After that, Upload is the action that moves a Local Set into the Cloud section."},{"type":"prose","html":"All three still require internet. All three still use the same eight slots and the same email. There is no second, hidden Cloud. If upload is missing, Cloud is off or the Set is already in Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one enable path and one upload path and stick to them. For this course: enable from App Settings, upload from Set Options."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open App Settings and point at the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle.","Return to the library and point at the <strong>Enable Ableton Cloud</strong> area if it is visible.","Open Set Options on a Local Set and point at <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>.","If you still need a test upload and you have a free slot, use Set Options on a disposable duplicate — not on a Demo.","After upload, confirm that Set now sits in the Cloud section."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the three official Cloud entry points in Note. <strong>Answer:</strong> App Settings toggle, Enable Ableton Cloud in the Set Library, and Upload to Ableton Cloud in Set Options.","Which of those sends a specific Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Options → <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>.","Do the three entry points create three different Cloud accounts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They are one service on one email."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, How do I enable Ableton Cloud in Note?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Without uploading extra Sets, touch all three entry points so you can find them cold. Then, if Gate A still needs a test upload, use Set Options on one Set only."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-6","title":"1.6.6 — Offline queue when network returns","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.6 — Offline queue when network returns","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start or retry a Cloud upload knowing Note needs a network, and that a queued upload begins when a connection returns."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note requires an internet connection to upload Sets. If mobile/cellular data or Wi-Fi is turned off, uploads begin automatically once a connection is available. That is the official queue. You do not babysit a progress wheel on airplane mode. You request the upload, restore the network, and wait."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can force Wi-Fi-only Cloud from the <em>device</em> Settings: <strong>Settings → Apps → Note</strong> and switch off <strong>Mobile/Cellular Data</strong>. Cloud then transfers only when Wi-Fi is on. That is useful on a metered plan. It is also why an upload “never starts” if you disabled cellular and you are not on Wi-Fi."},{"type":"prose","html":"The troubleshooting article adds two timing facts. Note can only upload or remove Sets while the device is on Wi-Fi or mobile data. Upload time ranges from a few seconds to a few minutes depending on Set size and connection. Sync can look finished in Note after the upload even though download to other devices (including Live) is still running. File size and internet speed control that second hop."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not show a labeled “offline queue” list or a retry button. Trust the automatic start when the network returns, then confirm the Set appears in the Cloud section and, later, in Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note is allowed to use Wi-Fi, and cellular if you want uploads off Wi-Fi.","Choose <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> on a small test Set (or note that an earlier upload is pending).","If you are offline, restore Wi-Fi or cellular and wait. Do not spam the toggle.","Watch the Set move into the Cloud section on Note.","Only then look in Live. If Live is empty, wait; download can still be in progress."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What happens to an upload if Wi-Fi and cellular are off? <strong>Answer:</strong> It begins automatically once a connection is available.","How do you stop Cloud from using cellular data? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS Settings → Apps → Note → turn off Mobile/Cellular Data.","If Note shows the Set in Cloud but Live does not yet, what might still be happening? <strong>Answer:</strong> Download to the computer may not have finished. Size and internet speed affect that time."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (Mobile Data toggle)","Note Sets in Ableton Cloud not Appearing in Live, Step 1","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (sync time vs “complete” in Note)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you can do it safely, disable Wi-Fi and cellular, request an upload on a disposable Set, then restore the network and wait for the Cloud section to update. If you cannot go offline, just write the auto-start rule from memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-7","title":"1.6.7 — Multi-device same account","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.7 — Multi-device same account","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use Cloud to move a Set between several of your own devices by signing every copy of Note (and Live) into the same email."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you are logged into Ableton Cloud with your ableton.com account on multiple devices, transferred Sets show up in the Cloud section on every device that has Note installed. The FAQ says the same: Sets synced in Cloud transfer automatically to any iOS device logged into Cloud with the same email."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the multi-device rule. Same email, Cloud enabled on each Note, internet on each device. The Set occupies one of the eight slots, not one slot per device. Edits are not documented as a real-time merge. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not describe conflict resolution if you change the same Cloud Set on two iPads at once. Work on one device, wait for sync, then open it on the next."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live is another “device” in this picture: Cloud in Places, same email, Live 11.2.5+. Consent for usage data also syncs across Note instances when Cloud is enabled. That is privacy plumbing, not a music feature, but it confirms that Cloud identity is global for that account."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a second iPhone shows an empty Cloud, it is almost always a different email or Cloud still off. Re-enable. Do not Share To… to yourself unless you <em>want</em> a separate local copy that is not in the eight-slot pool."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On device A, confirm Cloud is on and a test Set is in the Cloud section.","On device B (if you have one), install or open Note, enable Cloud, sign in with the same email.","Wait for the Cloud section on B to list that Set. Open it only after it appears.","If you have only one iOS device, treat Live as device B and look under Cloud in Places.","Write the match list: same email, Cloud on, internet, one shared eight-slot tray."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What makes a Set appear on a second iPhone’s Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> That iPhone is logged into Ableton Cloud with the same email, with Cloud enabled.","Does each device get its own eight slots? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud holds eight Sets for the account.","Is Live part of that same-account group? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, when Cloud is shown and signed in on Live 11.2.5+."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options (multiple devices)","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (other Note instances, same registered email)","Note FAQ, Can I transfer Note Sets from one iOS device to another?","Ableton Note Manual §2.6 (usage data consent synced when Cloud is enabled)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you own two iOS devices, enable Cloud on both with one email and confirm the test Set appears. If not, confirm the same Set in Live’s Cloud folder. Either check counts as multi-endpoint sync."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-8","title":"1.6.8 — Move/Push Sets appearing in Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.8 — Move/Push Sets appearing in Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expect Sets that originated on Move (and Cloud-connected Push/Note devices) to show up in Note’s Cloud area on the same account."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud is not a Note-only tray. The manual: Cloud stores up to eight Sets from <strong>Note and Move</strong>, accessible across Ableton products. Once Cloud is on in Note, Sets already stored in Cloud — “for example, from Move” — appear in the Ableton Cloud area of the Set Library. The transfer chapter says you share Note Sets with Live, Move, Push, or other devices that have Note installed."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.2 added sharing Sets with Ableton Move and raised the cap to eight. If a Move Set you did not create on this iPhone suddenly appears, that is the feature. It still consumes a slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The local extracts do not document a full Move or Push authoring workflow, nor a dedicated “this came from Push” badge. Treat incoming Cloud rows as Sets you can open in Note or Live. Do not invent extra Move-only Cloud rules here; Appendix A covers the Move peer relationship."},{"type":"prose","html":"Artwork on a Set that originated on Move had a Note 1.2.1 fix: adding artwork required a Note restart before it showed. If an old Move Set’s picture is stale, restart Note. That is a display quirk, not a missing file."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not delete a Move Set from Cloud to tidy the iPhone list unless you intend to free that slot for everyone on the account."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable Cloud if it is not on.","Scan the Cloud section for Sets you did not create on this device.","Preview one of those rows if any exist. Do not delete it.","Count total Cloud rows toward the shared eight.","If the Cloud section is only your Note Sets, write “no Move/Push Sets on this account” and move on."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Whose Sets share the eight Cloud slots with Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Move Sets (and other Cloud-connected Ableton products on the account).","What happens in Note after Cloud is activated if Move already uploaded Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Those Sets appear in the Ableton Cloud area of the Set Library.","Should you delete an unfamiliar Cloud Set to make the iPhone look tidy? <strong>Answer:</strong> No, not unless you mean to free that account slot."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Sets can be shared with Ableton Move; 8-set Cloud)","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (artwork on Move-originated Sets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"List every Set currently in Cloud and mark origin if you know it (this iPhone / another Note / Move / unknown). That list is your shared eight."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-9","title":"1.6.9 — Cloud warns at approximately 100 MB of sample data","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.9 — Cloud warns at approximately 100 MB of sample data","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize the 100 MB sample limit that Note warns about for Cloud, and stop a sample before it cannot be imported."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.6 added warnings when a sample exceeds the 100 MB Cloud limit. The same notes say samples larger than 100 MB cannot be imported or selected. That is a hard ceiling on the sample, not a soft “maybe.” If you try to bring a huge recording into Drum Rack or Sampler, Note should warn you and refuse the import or selection."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical consequence is practical: long phone videos, uncompressed live-show recordings, and huge stems can fail before they ever sit in a Set, which also means they never ride Cloud to Live. Crop or convert first. Import WAV, AIFF, M4A, or MP3; M4A and MP3 become WAV on import, which can grow the file. A 100 MB ceiling after conversion is the one that matters."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not state a total Set-size Cloud cap beyond this per-sample 100 MB warning, and they do not give a progress-bar UI description. Teach the sample limit. If an <em>upload</em> hangs, also check network, illegal name characters (colon), and Cloud status — those are separate failure modes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep Cloud transfers lean. Record short. Save the working sample into User Library only when you need it in other Sets. The 100 MB warning exists so Cloud stays a sketch transfer, not a film dump."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Before importing a long file, check its size in Files or on the computer.","If it is over 100 MB, crop or bounce a shorter version <em>before</em> you share it into Note.","Import a legal-size sample into a Sampler or Drum Rack only if you need one for this lesson.","If Note shows a 100 MB Cloud warning, dismiss it and use a smaller file. Do not keep retrying the same giant sample.","Recite the two 1.0.6 facts: warning at 100 MB, and samples larger than 100 MB cannot be imported or selected."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 1.0.6 add around Cloud and sample size? <strong>Answer:</strong> Warnings when a sample exceeds the 100 MB Cloud limit.","Can you import a sample larger than 100 MB? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Samples larger than 100 MB cannot be imported or selected.","Name two other reasons a Cloud upload might fail besides size. <strong>Answer:</strong> No network; illegal characters such as a colon in the Set name; Cloud server status."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (100 MB Cloud warning; cannot import/select samples over 100 MB)","Working with Samples in Note; Note FAQ (import formats WAV, AIFF, M4A, MP3)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (colon forbidden for Cloud)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do not import a 100 MB file on purpose. Check the size of any sample you already imported into LAB-01. If anything is huge, plan a shorter replacement before you rely on Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-10","title":"1.6.10 — Live version ≥ 11.2.5 for Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.10 — Live version ≥ 11.2.5 for Cloud","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm Live is 11.2.5 or later before you expect Cloud or an <code>.ablbundle</code> to open."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud is available in Live 11.2.5 and later. Note Sets themselves are supported in that same version floor, in every edition: Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, Education. A current Lite 12 that shipped with Note is fine. Live 11.2.4 or any older 11/10 install is not a Cloud target."},{"type":"prose","html":"Check the version on the computer: <strong>Live → About Live</strong> (macOS) or the equivalent Help/About command on Windows. If you are below 11.2.5, update Live before you blame Note, email, or the internet. The compatibility article points at Ableton’s “How to update Live” guide."},{"type":"prose","html":"Edition does not matter. A Trial can open a Note Set. Suite is not required. Education is included. What matters is the version number. After Note 1.1.1, an old “new file format version” warning in Live was a Note metadata bug; re-save the Set from an updated Note. You do not need a newer Live for <em>that</em> false warning."},{"type":"prose","html":"Opening the Set is only half the job. Collect All and Save so the Live project owns its samples. Then you can stay on 11.2.5+ and ignore Cloud if you prefer <code>.ablbundle</code> via <strong>Share To…</strong>. Both paths share the same version floor."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Live and read <strong>About Live</strong>. Write the version.","If it is below 11.2.5, update before continuing Cloud tests.","Confirm the edition (Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, or Education). Any of those is allowed.","If you already claimed Lite from Note → Settings → <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>, authorize that license and recheck the version.","Say the floor from memory: 11.2.5, all editions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the minimum Live version for Cloud and Note Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> 11.2.5.","Which Live editions can open Note Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, and Education.","If Live shows a false “new file format version” warning on an older Note Set, what do you do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Re-save the Set from Note 1.1.1 or later. No Live update is required for that specific bug."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, What versions of Live work with Ableton Cloud?","Note Compatibility (11.2.5+; all editions and licenses)","Ableton Note Manual §3.3; §10.1","Note Release Notes 1.1.1 (false new-format warning)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write your Live version on a sticky. If you have no Live yet, claim Lite from Note Settings and install 11.2.5+ / Live 12 Lite before Gate A’s optional Live check."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-11","title":"1.6.11 — Show Cloud in Live Library settings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.11 — Show Cloud in Live Library settings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn <strong>Show Cloud</strong> on in Live’s Library settings so the Cloud label appears in Places."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Live will not show Note Sets until Cloud is visible in the browser. The FAQ: to disable Cloud in Live, go to <strong>Live → Settings → Library</strong> and turn off <strong>Show Cloud</strong>. The inverse is the setup step. Open Live’s Settings (Preferences on some wordings), open <strong>Library</strong>, and enable <strong>Show Cloud</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"A Cloud icon then appears in <strong>Places</strong>. Select it and sign in with the same email Note uses. Uploaded Sets appear as folders: samples, recordings, a <code>.abl</code> file, and an <code>.ablbundle</code>. Double-click the bundle or expand <code>.abl</code> to reach tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"If <strong>Show Cloud</strong> is off, troubleshooting the phone is wasted time. The Sets can be in Cloud and still be invisible in Live. Turn the option on, sign in, wait for download. File size and internet speed still apply."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts use both “Settings” and “Preferences” for Live’s window depending on the document. On your Live version, open the Preferences/Settings window and find the <strong>Library</strong> tab. The control name to learn is <strong>Show Cloud</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is hide/show in the Live browser, not an account opt-out. The next lesson separates those."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Live’s Settings / Preferences → <strong>Library</strong>.","Enable <strong>Show Cloud</strong>.","In the browser, find <strong>Cloud</strong> under Places and select it.","Sign in if prompted, using the Live license email.","Confirm any Sets you uploaded from Note appear as folders. If the list is empty, wait, then continue to the troubleshooting lesson before re-uploading."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is <strong>Show Cloud</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Live → Settings (Preferences) → Library.","Where do uploaded Note Sets appear after that? <strong>Answer:</strong> Under <strong>Cloud</strong> in Places in Live’s browser.","If Show Cloud is off, can you still see Note Sets in Places? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Turn it on first."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Can I turn off Ableton Cloud?","Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (Cloud folder in Places)","Note Sets in Ableton Cloud not Appearing in Live, Step 2","Ableton Note.md §5.1 (Preferences &gt; Library &gt; Show Cloud)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn <strong>Show Cloud</strong> on, sign in, and look for your test Set. If you are not at a computer, write the menu path and do this before you call Cloud broken."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-12","title":"1.6.12 — Disable Cloud on Note vs hide in Live","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.12 — Disable Cloud on Note vs hide in Live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn Cloud off for one Note install, hide Cloud in Live’s browser, and understand that neither action wipes Cloud data by itself."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Two different off switches exist."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note, go to <strong>Settings → Ableton Cloud</strong> and turn the toggle off. That disables Cloud for <em>that device</em>. Other Note installs and Live can stay signed in. You still have Local Sets. You just stopped this iPhone from uploading or removing Cloud Sets."},{"type":"prose","html":"In Live, <strong>Settings → Library</strong> → turn off <strong>Show Cloud</strong>. That hides the Cloud label in Places. It does not opt the account out of Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"The troubleshooting article is stricter about deletion: disabling Cloud on all iOS devices does <strong>not</strong> remove already-synced Sets from Live’s browser. Only by opting out completely via your Ableton Account will data be removed from Cloud. {type:'gap'} The step-by-step account opt-out lives in a “How to Disable Ableton Cloud” article that is not in this extract folder. Do not invent those website clicks. Know that device toggles ≠ wipe."},{"type":"prose","html":"Temporary hide is useful when you are teaching, when you want Live’s browser quieter, or when you are on a device that should not touch the eight slots. Wipe is a rare account-level decision."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turning Note’s toggle off and on is also the start of the re-enable login flow when emails do not match."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Note, point at <strong>Settings → Ableton Cloud</strong> and say “this device only.”","In Live, point at <strong>Settings → Library → Show Cloud</strong> and say “hide in Places.”","Leave both on if you still need Gate A’s optional upload.","If you must hide Live’s Cloud for a while, turn <strong>Show Cloud</strong> off, then on again later. Do not expect Sets to vanish from the account.","Write the third layer you will <em>not</em> perform in this course: full Ableton Account opt-out."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you disable Cloud on one iPhone? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note → Settings → Ableton Cloud toggle off.","How do you hide Cloud in Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Live → Settings → Library → Show Cloud off.","Does turning Cloud off on every iOS device delete already-synced Sets from Live’s browser? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Only a full account opt-out removes Cloud data."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Can I turn off Ableton Cloud?","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (disabling on iOS does not remove Live browser Sets)","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do not wipe your account. Leave Cloud enabled on the Note you use for this course. If Live’s browser feels crowded, you may hide <strong>Show Cloud</strong> after you have confirmed a test Set once."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-13","title":"1.6.13 — Troubleshooting: Cloud not in Live browser","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.13 — Troubleshooting: Cloud not in Live browser","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Run the official four-step check when a Note Set is in Cloud on the phone but missing from Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Work the troubleshooting article in order. Do not skip to “reinstall everything.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Step 1 — Internet on the iOS device.</strong> Note can only upload or remove Sets on Wi-Fi or mobile data. In iOS Settings confirm <strong>Wi-Fi</strong> or <strong>Mobile Data</strong> (and that Note is allowed to use cellular). Upload time is seconds to minutes depending on Set size and connection."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Step 2 — Cloud enabled on both products, same email.</strong> Enable Cloud in Note and in Live. The accounts must match."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Step 3 — Re-enable both sides.</strong> Toggle Cloud off/on in Note, log out of ableton.com in the mobile browser if the wrong account auto-filled, sign in with the Live license email, return via <strong>Open</strong>. In Live, right-click Cloud → <strong>Sign Out</strong>, sign in again from Places, log out of the desktop browser if needed, then <strong>Return to Application</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Step 4 — Cloud server status.</strong> A temporary outage can make Sets look unsynced. Check Ableton’s Status page (Note 1.1.2 put a Status link in settings)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Also remember: Note can show the upload as done while Live is still downloading. After you remove a Set from Cloud while Live is closed, Live may still <em>temporarily</em> list it when reopened. Skip projects missing their song file (RN 1.0.15). Sets with no artwork had a sync bug that was fixed. Illegal colon in the name can block Cloud. Live must be 11.2.5+ with <strong>Show Cloud</strong> on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm the Set is in Note’s Cloud section.","Confirm the iPhone/iPad is online and Note may use that network.","Confirm Live ≥ 11.2.5, <strong>Show Cloud</strong> on, same email as Note.","If still missing, run the re-enable flow on Note and Live, then wait for download.","If still missing, open the Status page. Only then consider a fresh <strong>Share To…</strong> <code>.ablbundle</code> as a bypass, not as a “Cloud fix.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the first check when Cloud Sets do not appear in Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Internet connectivity on the iOS device, including Note’s Mobile Data permission.","What is the most common account mistake? <strong>Answer:</strong> Different emails on Note and Live.","Can Note report the Set as uploaded while Live is still empty? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Download to Live can still be in progress."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Sets in Ableton Cloud not Appearing in Live (Steps 1–4)","Troubleshooting Problems with Note Files (sync delay; stale Cloud label)","Note FAQ, My sets in Ableton Cloud aren’t showing up in Live","Note Release Notes 1.1.2 (Status page); 1.0.12 (colon); 1.0.15 (Cloud fixes)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If your test Set is already visible in Live, write the four steps from memory anyway. If it is not visible, run the four steps once, in order, and log what changed."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-14","title":"1.6.14 — Live Sets cannot be transferred into Note","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.14 — Live Sets cannot be transferred into Note","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Plan every Cloud and Share To… hand-off as Note → Live, because a Live Set cannot come back."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sharing between Note and Live works in one direction. The manual: Sets from Note can be opened in Live, but not the other way around. The FAQ: it is not possible to transfer Sets from Live into Note. Once a Note Set is imported into Live and saved as a Live Set, the modified version can no longer be opened in Note. Compatibility article: Sets created or saved in Live cannot be opened in Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the professional boundary. Sketch and capture on the phone. Open the <code>.ablbundle</code> or Cloud folder on the computer. Arrange, mix, and finish in Live. If you need another mobile sketch, start a <em>new</em> Note Set. Do not expect the Live arrangement, extra tracks, or Live-only devices to round-trip."},{"type":"prose","html":"A related, narrower path exists for <em>presets</em>, not Sets: after you open a Note Set in Live you can save a Note preset into Live’s User Library. Only specifically created presets can be exported from Live and transferred to Note (Move &amp; Note preset rules). That is not “open this Live Set in Note.” Do not smuggle a Live Set through Cloud and hope."},{"type":"prose","html":"Treat the phone file as the sketch. Treat the Live project after <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> as a new work. Keep the Note Set in <strong>Projects</strong> if you still want the mobile version."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Say the one-way rule out loud: Note → Live, never Live → Note.","If you already opened LAB-01 in Live, save that as a Live project with <strong>Collect All and Save</strong>.","Re-open LAB-01 in Note and confirm it is still the phone sketch, not the Live arrangement.","Do not look for an “Upload to Note” command in Live. It is not there.","Write one sentence you will use at the end of a session: “Finish in Live; start a new Set on the phone.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you open a Live Set in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Sets created or saved in Live cannot be opened in Note.","What happens after you import a Note Set into Live and save it as a Live Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> That modified Live version can no longer be opened in Note.","Is Cloud two-way? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud transfers Note Sets to Live / Note / Move / Push on the same account, not Live Sets back into Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §10.1 (one direction)","Note FAQ, Can I transfer Sets from Live to Note via Ableton Cloud?","Note FAQ, Can I transfer Live Sets back into Note once I've worked on them in Live?","Note Compatibility (Sets created or saved in Live cannot be opened in Note)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Look at LAB-01 in Note and, if you have it, the Live project you collected. They are two files now. Do not try to merge them backward. Gate A only needs the Note side plus an optional Cloud upload."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l1-6-15","title":"1.6.15 — Gate A assessment","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.15 — Gate A assessment","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 1 · Module 1.6 — Ableton Cloud deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-score Level 1 on your own Set: create, identify, back up, and optionally upload, using only facts from this level."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate A is a checklist, not software. Score yourself on a Set you created. Do not use a Demo as the test file. Every item below is a Level 1 fact already sourced. If an item fails, reopen that lesson and retry before Level 2."},{"type":"prose","html":"Mark <strong>Pass</strong> or <strong>Fail</strong> on paper:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Create.</strong> <strong>+ New</strong> makes a Set with three default tracks and opens Session View.","<strong>Return.</strong> Back arrow left of the Set name returns to the Sets Library (from Instrument View it returns to Session first).","<strong>Identity.</strong> You can point at Local vs Cloud vs Demo and preview with the play button without opening.","<strong>Name and picture.</strong> Set Options → <strong>Rename</strong> (no colon) and artwork from Photos or camera.","<strong>Safety copy.</strong> A <strong>Duplicate</strong> named <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01-BACKUP</strong> exists, or an <code>.ablbundle</code> exists in Files.","<strong>Files path.</strong> You can open <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note</strong> and find <strong>Projects</strong>.","<strong>Auto-save.</strong> You can state the 10–15 second interval and that Cloud is not a backup.","<strong>Share To…</strong> You know it writes an <code>.ablbundle</code> (Set + samples + metadata) for Note or Live 11.2.5+.","<strong>Cloud rules.</strong> Eight Sets; same email as the Live license; transfer not archive; Note → Live only.","<strong>Optional upload.</strong> One test Set in the Cloud section <em>or</em> you wrote why you skipped Cloud (no account / no slot / no Live yet)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"No auto-grader. No invented extra criteria. Optional upload is optional."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>NOTE-COURSE-LAB-01</strong>, not a Demo.","Work the ten items in order. Write Pass or Fail beside each.","For any Fail, do the action once more from the matching lesson.","Preview LAB-01, open it, return to the library, and find it again in Files.","Stop Level 1 only when required items 1–9 are Pass. Item 10 may be Pass or an explicit skip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What Set do you use for Gate A? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Set you created (LAB-01), not a Demo.","Which Cloud fact must you be able to state even if you skip the upload? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cloud holds eight Sets, uses the Live-license email, is transfer not backup, and is Note → Live only.","Who scores Gate A? <strong>Answer:</strong> You. It is a self-check of Level 1 facts."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §3.1–3.3, §4, §10.1","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud; Managing Files in Note","Sharing Note Sets","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (colon); 1.0.6 (100 MB); 1.2 (eight Sets)","This level’s lessons 1.1.1–1.6.14"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the ten-line pass/fail list. Photograph it or keep it with LAB-01. Do not start Level 2 until items 1–9 pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l1-6-15</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
